{"parentUuid":"2a075a10-fddb-452b-b9bb-cf54c6ffd827","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NFNjx3aLELovW1nySENMDN","type":"tool_result","content":"#type: include\r\n\r\n== Border == (border)\r\n\r\nBorder:\r\n    #id: border\r\n\r\n    The sampling behavior outside of the incoming layer's boundaries. Automatically uses the layer's border property. \r\n    \r\n    See [Border types|/copernicus/glossary#border] for more information.\r\n\r\n== Mask == (mask)\r\n\r\nMask:\r\n    #id: mask\r\n\r\n    The amount of the new value to mix in with the original value. Higher values mix in more of the new value. Lower values mix in less of the new value. You can also set the RGBA [channels|Wp:Channel_(digital_image)] to which you apply the effects of this node.\r\n\r\n    The default value is `1`, which means this node uses only the new value. See [Mask|/copernicus/glossary#mask] for more information.\r\n\r\n    Toggle RGB:\r\n        Turns the Red, Green, and Blue channels on and off.\r\n\r\n    Red:\r\n        Applies this node's effects to the Red channel.\r\n\r\n    Green:\r\n        Applies this node's effects to the Green channel.\r\n\r\n    Blue:\r\n        Applies this node's effects to the Blue channel.\r\n\r\n    Alpha:\r\n        Applies this node's effects to the Alpha channel.\r\n\r\n== Precision == (precision)\r\n\r\nPrecision:\r\n    #id: precision\r\n\r\n    The layer's storage precision. This controls how many bytes of storage each channel of the layer requires. More bytes allow for larger integer values or more accuracy for floating point values, but require more memory.\r\n\r\n    8:\r\n        The channel uses 8-bits and defaults to quarter-precision.\r\n\r\n    16:\r\n        The channel uses 16-bits and defaults to half-precision.\r\n\r\n    32:\r\n        The channel uses 32-bits and defaults to full-precision.\r\n\r\n== Use Fixed Point Storage == (fixed_point)\r\n\r\nUse Fixed Point Storage:\r\n    #id: usefixedpoint\r\n\r\n    Stores 8-bit unsigned and 16-bit signed floating point values as fixed point values. This clamps 8-bit fixed point values to the `0` to `1` range and 16-bit fixed point values to the `-1` to `1` range. This parameter applies to only non-ID layers.\r\n    \r\n    Storage types are propagated through the network, so nodes often use the same storage type as their inputs. Fixed point storage offers more consistent precision for the same amount of storage compared to floating point, but can only represent values in a limited range.\r\n    \r\n    TIP:\r\n        Hold ((MMB)) on the node to use the [node info window|/network/info] to check the storage type of each layer.\r\n\r\n== Post Processing == (post_processing)\r\n\r\nNOTE:\r\n    The node applies the post-processing operations in the order they're listed.\r\n\r\nFold:\r\n    #id: post_dofold\r\n    \r\n    Sets the noise values to the absolute value. For example, a pixel value of `-2` becomes `2`.\r\n\r\n    NOTE:\r\n        This parameter doesn't impact the output when there's only positive values.\r\n\r\nComplement:\r\n    #id: post_docomplement\r\n\r\n    Sets the pixel value `x` to `1 - x`.\r\n\r\n    NOTE:\r\n        This method works for values in the `0` to `1` range. For images outside of this range, using `Complement` results in negative or out of range values.\r\n\r\nBias:\r\n    #id: post_bias\r\n\r\n    The amount to pull the medium grey values (around `0.5`) towards zero (if __Bias__ is less than `1`) or one (if __Bias__ is greater than `1`). A bias value of `0.5` doesn't affect the values.\r\n    [Image:/images/nodes/cop/postprocessing_bias.jpg]\r\n\r\nGain:\r\n    #id: post_gain\r\n\r\n    The amount to pull the medium grey values (around `0.5`) together, while values around `0` and `1` are pulled apart. A gain value of `0.5` doesn't affect the noise values.\r\n    [Image:/images/nodes/cop/postprocessing_gain.jpg]\r\n\r\nGamma:\r\n    #id: post_gamma\r\n\r\n    The overall gamma of the generated noise. Values greater than `1` increase the range of values in originally bright areas, which darkens the noise. Values less than `1` stretch out the range of values for originally dark areas, which increases the overall brightness of the noise.\r\n\r\nContrast:\r\n    #id: post_contrast\r\n\r\n    The amount to expand or shrink the overall range of tonal values. Each noise value is pushed towards (if __Contrast__ is less than `1`) or away from (if __Contrast__ is greater than `1`) medium grey values (around `0.5`).\r\n\r\n    NOTE:\r\n        This is not the same as __Noise Contrast__, which appears in some noise nodes and applies contrast during the noise function.\r\n\r\nClamp Minimum:\r\n    #id: post_minimum\r\n\r\n    Clamps values below the specified threshold.\r\n\r\nClamp Maximum:\r\n    #id: post_maximum\r\n    \r\n    Clamps values exceeding the specified threshold.\r\n\r\n== Rename Wires Using Output Labels == (cablerenamebylabel)\r\n\r\nRename Wires Using Output Labels:\r\n    #id: cablerenamebylabel\r\n\r\n    When this toggle is enabled, name of each output wire is set based on its\r\n    respective port label. Otherwise, wire names are propagated from inputs.\r\n    Turn this option on if you'd like to discard incoming wire names and harden\r\n    new ones for the downstream network to use.\r\n\r\n~~~~~~~~~~\n#type: include\n\n== Mask == (mask)\n\nmask:\n    An optional per-pixel mask amount that's scaled by the __Mask__ parameter.\n\n== Size_ref == (size_ref)\n\nsize_ref:\n    A representative layer that determines the size of the output image and controls the metadata.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"28b613fe-f7ff-424b-8ce4-712bab7f4035","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:25:35.043Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d3f9afaf-ea9e-4102-88c9-8f37edfad96e","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_br