{"parentUuid":"1b17929e-bf41-438c-8d2f-a36191c78c99","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfvc6nyWch1Ukg5oRdLD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0194oce2RedST7dqtJwHeHRB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/hou-help-cop/cop && sed -n 1,200p cellularnoise.txt | cut -c1-330","description":"Read the cellularnoise COP help"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4067,"cache_read_input_tokens":380557,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4067,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0194oce2RedST7dqtJwHeHRB":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/hou-help-cop/cop && sed -n 1,200p cellularnoise.txt | cut -c1-330","description":"Read the cellularnoise COP help"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"bfdb6f75-a104-4c5a-bdad-ea407fe1c02f","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfvc6nev1WVuR5rba3Xbc","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"df6dd326-8a71-47b4-871c-0b668c75287b","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:19.870Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"df6dd326-8a71-47b4-871c-0b668c75287b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0194oce2RedST7dqtJwHeHRB","type":"tool_result","content":"#type:     node\n#context:  cop\n#internal: cellularnoise\n#icon:     COP/cellularnoise\n#since:    22.0\n\n= Cellular Noise =\n\n\"\"\"Generates cellular noise.\"\"\"\n\nThis node uses [Worley noise|Wp:Worley_noise] to generate cellular patterns with controlled spacing and beveling. Use the [Cellular Noise 3D COP|Node:/cop/cellularnoise3d] if you want the source location to be the pixels' world coordinates instead of its image coordinates.\n\n[Image:/images/nodes/cop/cellular_noise.jpg]\n\nFor example, use this node to create patterns that resemble skin, leather, cobblestone, pebbles, or dried earth.\n\n@parameters\n\nType Info:\n    #id: typeinfo\n    \n    The type information for the resulting layer.\n\n    See [Type info|/copernicus/glossary#type_info] for more information.\n\n== Range ==\n\nAmplitude:\n    #id: amplitude\n\n    A factor controlling the noise's strength or intensity. Higher values increase the contrast between pattern elements; lower values reduce it.\n    \nShift:    \n    #id: shift\n    \n    The amount to increase or decrease the layer's pixel value. Higher values increase the pixel value and overall brightness. Lower values decrease the pixel value and overall brightness.\n\n\n== Pattern ==\n\nElement Size:\n    #id: elementsize\n    \n    The size (in [image coordinates|/copernicus/spaces]) of the basic element of the noise. You can turn on the [Smallicon:BUTTONS/decompose_transforms] __Per-Component Controls__ option to adjust this further using the __Element Scale__ parameter.\n    \nElement Scale:\n    #id: elementscale\n\n    When you turn on the [Smallicon:BUTTONS/decompose_transforms] __Per-Component Controls__ option, this parameter scales the element size per axis to create anisotropic noise.\n    \nJitter:\n    #id: jitter\n    \n    The amount of random noise to add to each point's position on a grid. The random noise is added before the Worley noise measures the distance to the points. You can turn on the [Smallicon:BUTTONS/decompose_transforms] __Per-Component Controls__ option to adjust this further using the __Jitter Scale__ parameter.\n    \n    NOTE:\n        Values greater than `1` may produce artifacts because they jitter past the search radius. \n    \nJitter Scale:\n    #id: jitterscale\n    \n    When you turn on [Smallicon:BUTTONS/decompose_transforms] __Per-Component Controls__ option, this is the per-axis scaling of the jitter.\n    \n    \nLattice Type:\n    #id: lattice\n\n    The grid to define Worley noise on and determine the cell shape.\n\n    Grid:\n        Define Worley noise on a rectangular grid to create square base shapes. This is the common option.\n\n    Hexagonal:\n        Define Worley noise on a so-called simplicial grid to create hexagonal base shapes. For higher computational efficiency, simplicial grids are made of triangles or tetrahedrons instead of regular cubes or squares.\n\nMetric:\n    #id: metric\n    \n    The metric for computing the distance to a point.\n    \n    Euclidean:\n        Use the length of the line segment between the two axial points to define their distance. This can result in circular shapes. See [Euclidean distance|Wp:Euclidean_distance] for more information.\n        \n    Manhattan:\n        Use the sum of the differences between the two axial points to define their distance. This can result in diamond shapes. See [Taxicab geometry|Wp:Taxicab_geometry] for more information.\n\n    Chebyshev:\n        Use the maximum difference between the two axial points to define their distance. This can result in square shapes. See [Chebyshev distance|Wp:Chebyshev_distance] for more information.\n\nSmooth Edge:\n    #id: smoothedge\n    \n    When __Metric__ is set to __Euclidean__, you can control the roundness of the cells.\n        \nOffset:\n    #id: off\n    \n    Shifts the entire noise function in image coordinates.\n    \nTile Size:\n    #id: tilesize\n\n    The size of a single tile of noise. The noise periodically repeats in this size. The size is in image coordinates, so the default covers the entire standard image space (\"canonical image\"). If you have a non-square image, the values should match the aspect ratio.\n\n    NOTE:\n        If on, values for parameters like __Element Size__ are rounded or clamped to make them valid. This is because these types of parameters must meet certain conditions for the noise to be tileable.\n    \n    \n== Shape ==\n\nMode:\n    #id: mode\n\n    Determines whether the shape parameters work as absolute values in [image Space|/copernicus/spaces], or as relative values in the 0 to 1 range.\n\n    Absolute:\n        Shape the cells using absolute values in image space.\n\n    Relative:\n        Shape the cells using normalized values that are 0 at the cell border and 1 at the cell center. When __Jitter__ is applied, a value of `1` corresponds to the most elongated cell that is possible.\n\nGap:\n    #id: gap, gap_relative\n    \n    The amount of space between each cell. Higher values increase the spacing between cells, and the cells will shrink until they're not visible.\n    \nBevel:\n    #id: bevel, bevel_relative\n    \n    The amount of bevel to add to the outer edges of each cell. Higher values taper the cells inwards to create a pointy top. Lower values decrease the slope to create a flat top with hard edges.\n    \n    TIP:\n        When __Metric__ is set to __Euclidean__, you can use this parameter in combination with __Smooth Edge__ to create pebble-like shapes.\n\nBevel Ramp:\n    #id: bevelramp\n\n    A ramp to adjust the shape of the bevel. The left side of the ramp corresponds to each cell's border, and the right side corresponds to the inset length given by the __Bevel__ parameter.\n    \n    \n== Post Processing ==\n:include /nodes/cop/_parameter_descriptions#post_processing/:  \n    \n@inputs\n    :include /nodes/cop/_input_descriptions#size_ref/:\n\n    pos:\n        An optional UV layer containing a value that's used instead of the pixel's image coordinates for the noise.\n\n@outputs\n    noise:\n    \tThe computed noise.\n\n    border:\n    \tAn SDF Mono layer containing the exact distance to the border\n        between the two closest points. Use this to create even width cell boundaries.\n\n    center:\n        A UV layer containing the image coordinates of the closest grid\n        point. This can be used by a [UV Sample COP|Node:cop/sample] to create a mosaic\n        effect.\n\n    id:\n       An ID layer containing the hash of the closest grid point. Note this can be negative.\n\n@related\n- [Node:cop/worleynoise]\n- [Node:cop/cellpattern]","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3881f1f9-05b6-4d0c-bac4-abb233f5dfb7","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:27:20.242Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"bfdb6f75-a104-4c5a-bdad-ea407fe1c02f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"df6dd326-8a71-47b4-871c-0b668c75287b","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
