{"parentUuid":"2586fc00-d9a1-4ecb-9668-f0450cb13d44","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfvc8th75V7yhSQFWnVtE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WTuLihgisNwRWsgQchZrfS","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/01a_noises.txt","content":"## perlin-noise\nname: Perlin Noise\nsrc: /noises/perlin-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Smooth, widely used gradient noise whose grayscale values vary continuously across the image, with a tileable grid controlled by Scale.\nk: Scale | Number of grid subdivisions per side, so higher values give smaller and denser features.\nk: Disorder | Moves the internal ingredients of the noise around, handy for variation or animation.\nk: Disorder speed | Distance travelled by the Disorder displacement per unit change, i.e. how fast it animates.\nk: Tile offset | Slides the visible window over the infinite noise plane to pick another crop of the same pattern.\nk: Non-square expansion | Keeps cells square on non-square canvases by extending the generation to the image bounds.\nuse: Soft base undulation for terrain, ground, plaster or clay heights; often warped or blended at low opacity to break flat areas before finer detail is added.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Perlin and {Fractal Type}=None for one octave; {Element Size} replaces Scale (size of one grid cell in image coordinates), {Offset} replaces Tile offset, {Tile Size} makes it repeat, and the Post Processing group ({Bias}, {Gain}, {Contrast}, {Clamp Minimum}, {Clamp Maximum}) shapes the histogram. Seeds are obtained by changing {Offset} or enabling {3D Noise} and moving the third coordinate (this plays the role of Disorder, UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## gaussian-noise\nname: Gaussian Noise\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Smooth blobby noise built from overlapping gradients whose black to white transitions follow a bell curve, giving soft rounded lumps.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles; larger means more and smaller lumps.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the internal pieces of the noise for variation or animation.\nk: Disorder anisotropy | Narrows the directions in which Disorder pushes the pieces (0 means any direction).\nk: Disorder anisotropy angle | Direction of the Disorder displacement once anisotropy is non-zero.\nk: Tile offset | Chooses which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Go-to soft lumpy height for dunes, snow drifts, mud, cloth bulges and the large-scale pass of rock or ground before sharper noises are layered on top.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution (smooth value-style noise) and {Max Octaves} of 1 to 3 gives the same soft lumps; a cheaper alternative is @randommono blurred with @blur then stretched with @equalize. Use {Element Size} for Scale and {Tile Size} for seamless tiling.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## gaussian-spots-1\nname: Gaussian Spots 1\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-spots-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian-noise variant with narrower gradients, so the lumps read as separate soft spots instead of one continuous cloud.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision; more tiles give denser spots.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which part of the infinite plane is shown.\nuse: Pitting, soft pores, blotchy dirt masks or low-amplitude surface bumps on leather, plaster and skin heights.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution, then tighten the lumps with {Gain} above 0.5 or {Contrast} above 1 in Post Processing; or @cellularnoise with {Smooth Edge} and {Bevel} for round pebbles.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## gaussian-spots-2\nname: Gaussian Spots 2\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-spots-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Like Gaussian Spots 1 but with narrower gradients and higher frequencies, giving a finer sprinkling of soft spots.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which part of the infinite plane is shown.\nuse: Fine grain, micro pitting and small speckle heights that sit on top of a larger form.\ncop: Same chain as Gaussian Spots 1 with a smaller {Element Size} on @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution) and stronger {Contrast}; for hard speckle use @randommono blurred slightly and thresholded with @remap ({Operation}=Threshold).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## plasma\nname: Plasma\nsrc: /noises/plasma/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian-noise relative with longer dark streaks that act as valleys; the Scale control keeps the result tileable.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect (integer steps keep the tiling intact).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift of the noise for small variations.\nk: Non Square Expansion | Compensates squash and stretch on non-square images.\nuse: Veined, marbled or crackled low relief, canyon-like valleys in rock and ice heights.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Hybrid Terrain or Terrain and {Fold} (Post Processing) to create valley lines, optionally warped by @distort using a second noise as the direction layer. This is an approximation of the look (UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## liquid\nname: Liquid\nsrc: /noises/liquid/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian noise that is warped by itself, producing smooth swirling, liquid-looking undulations.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for variation.\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the self-warp that creates the flowing look.\nuse: Wet mud, lava crust, melted wax or flowing surface relief used as a base height or as a warp driver.\ncop: @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution) feeding @distort twice: once as the source and once as the direction layer, with {Scale} on @distort acting as Warp Intensity.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fluid\nname: Fluid\nsrc: /noises/fluid/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Generates a flowing or falling fluid pattern, useful where liquid runs under gravity such as rain streaks or leaks.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for variation.\nk: Warp Intensity | Intensity of the warp that bends the streaks.\nk: Pattern Size | Size of the drip or streak pattern.\nuse: Vertical rain, leaks and drip streaks as masks or very shallow relief on walls and metal.\ncop: @grunge_drips (drip pattern; {Density}, {Distortion}, {Primary Trailing}) or @grunge_leakingstreaks; plain streaks can also be made by @streakblur on sparse @randommono dots.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## microscope-view\nname: Microscope View\nsrc: /noises/microscope-view/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Warped noise reminiscent of bacteria or organisms seen under a microscope.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the warp; negative values are possible.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variations.\nuse: Organic cell-like relief for alien skin, coral or moss-like micro heights.\ncop: @cellularnoise ({Smooth Edge} and {Bevel}) or @turingpatterns for organic blobs, then @distort with a smooth noise as the direction layer for the warp (approximate, UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## creased\nname: Creased\nsrc: /noises/creased/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Cloth-like creased heightmap with a semi-directional look and large-scale variation.\nk: Scale | Global scale (integer 1 to 8).\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the bend and warp that makes the creases (0 to 128).\nk: Disorder | Slightly offsets the layers used to build the noise (0 to 100).\nuse: Wrinkled fabric, crumpled paper or foil, leather folds, rock strata creases.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Sharp and {Fold} in Post Processing for crease lines, then bend with @distort (direction layer from a low-frequency noise) to reproduce the Warp Intensity.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## crystal-1\nname: Crystal 1\nsrc: /noises/crystal-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Worley/Voronoi-style noise with a slightly angular distance metric, useful for geometric and faceted patterns.\nk: Scale | Global scale (1 to 256).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variations.\nuse: Crystals, faceted rock, ice shards, broken glass and hammered metal facets in height maps.\ncop: @crystalnoise (sharp, angular Worley type) with {Primary Metric}=Euclidean or Manhattan, {Element Size} for Scale and {Flatten Faces} to get flat facets; optionally add the {Secondary} layer.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## crystal-2\nname: Crystal 2\nsrc: /noises/crystal-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Angular, cloth-crease-like pattern similar to Creased; a niche noise for subtle marble veining or fabric detail.\nk: Scale | Global scale (1 to 16).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variations.\nuse: Fine marble veins, cloth creases and subtle faceted breakup.\ncop: @crystalnoise with {Secondary} enabled and {Secondary Metric}=Manhattan, plus {Fold} in Post Processing for the crease look (approximate).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## cells-1\nname: Cells 1\nsrc: /noises/cells-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Scatters a selectable shape in every grid cell and overlays the shapes with a Max blend, producing varied cell-like blobs.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision; more cells and smaller shapes at higher values.\nk: Pattern | The base shape scattered in each cell.\nk: Pattern size | Size multiplier per axis relative to the cell (1.0 spans the whole cell).\nk: Pattern scale | Uniform multiplier on top of Pattern size.\nk: Luminance random | Range of random darkening per cell, 1 being the full range.\nk: Angle | Direction of the shapes in turns, starting from horizontal right.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle, in turns.\nuse: Pebbles, cobblestones, scales, bumps and cell-like relief; the per-cell luminance random gives height variation between stones.\ncop: @cellularnoise (use the {noise} output, {Jitter}, {Bevel}, {Smooth Edge}, {Mode}=Relative) or @cellpattern which adds per-cell {Random Height}, {Rotate} and {Distort}; for arbitrary stamp shapes use @shapescatter.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## cells-2\nname: Cells 2\nsrc: /noises/cells-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Binary mask of Voronoi-like cells whose wall thickness can be adjusted.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the cells.\nk: Edge width | Wall thickness between cells as a ratio of the grid (resolution independent).\nk: Invert | Swaps black and white.\nuse: Crack networks, tile joints, dried mud plates and stone-wall mortar masks; subtract it from a height to carve the joints.\ncop: @cellularnoise, take the {border} output (an SDF mono layer of the exact distance to the cell border) and threshold it with @remap ({Operation}=Threshold, {Threshold When}=Less, {Threshold}=half the wall width) so the wall width stays even.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## cells-3\nname: Cells 3\nsrc: /noises/cells-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Intersection of discs, producing cells with thin walls of uneven softness.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the cells.\nk: Hardness | How crisp the cell walls are; higher is sharper.\nk: Invert | Inverts the grayscale values.\nk: Pattern size | Size of each scattered disc relative to the cell.\nk: Angle | Direction of the discs, in turns.\nuse: Soft cracked mud, organic cell walls, pores and cobweb-like relief with varying wall depth.\ncop: @cellularnoise {border} output remapped with @remap ({Use Ramp} on to control softness) or with @cellpattern and a {Bevel Ramp} for uneven wall softness.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## cells-4\nname: Cells 4\nsrc: /noises/cells-4/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Fills each cell with one flat value, either random or sampled from an input image at the cell location.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the cells.\nk: Color source | Where the flat value comes from: random seed, separate pseudorandom seed, or the input image.\nk: Pseudorandom seed | Seed for the color, independent of the node seed.\nuse: Per-cell random heights (cobbles, tiles, plates), or a mosaic of a source image used as a stepped height.\ncop: @cellularnoise: its {id} output into @idtomono or @randommono for random flat values per cell, and its {center} UV output into @sample (UV Sample) to read an input image at each cell center for a mosaic.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## clouds-1\nname: Clouds 1\nsrc: /noises/clouds-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: First of three cloud-style rough noises built from the same Disorder-based controls; the documentation page describes no visual difference between the three.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Large-scale soft relief, cloud cover masks and organic grunge bases; compare the three variants visually before choosing.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Terrain or Dampened, {Max Octaves} about 6 and {Roughness} about 0.5, or @cloudnoise3d / @bubblenoise for billowy results; the exact look match to this variant is UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## clouds-2\nname: Clouds 2\nsrc: /noises/clouds-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Second cloud-style rough noise with the same controls as Clouds 1; the page documents no visual difference to the other variants.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Same roles as Clouds 1 (large soft relief, dirt masks); pick by preview.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Terrain, more {Max Octaves} for a busier variant; match is approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## clouds-3\nname: Clouds 3\nsrc: /noises/clouds-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Third cloud-style rough noise with the same controls; the page documents no visual difference to the other variants.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Same roles as Clouds 1 and 2; useful as a mask to modulate erosion or moss coverage.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Hybrid Terrain for a more contrasted variant; match approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fractal-sum-base\nname: Fractal Sum Base\nsrc: /noises/fractal-sum-base/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Customisable fractal noise (sum of octaves) with an adjustable octave range and balance; the Fractal Sum 1 to 4 nodes are all based on it.\nk: Roughness | Balance between octaves; higher values make the high-frequency octaves more visible.\nk: Min. level | First octave used; higher means a higher base frequency.\nk: Max. level | Last octave used; higher adds finer detail.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the noise ingredients for variation or animation.\nk: Contrast | Contrast of the final result.\nk: Global opacity | Opacity of the summed octaves; a high value can saturate the result.\nuse: The universal height base: set low Min. level for continents or large dunes, higher Max. level for rock grain; combine with Histogram Scan to fix the range.\ncop: @fractalnoise: {Max Octaves} = Max. level minus Min. level plus 1, {Element Size} sets the lowest octave size (Min. level), {Roughness} per octave amplitude, {Lacunarity} frequency step, {Fractal Type}=Terrain/Dampened/Sharp/Hybrid Terrain, {Noise Contrast} or {Contrast} for contrast.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fractal-sum-1\nname: Fractal Sum 1\nsrc: /noises/fractal-sum-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Preset variation of Fractal Sum Base with fixed octave settings and only Disorder controls exposed.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the noise ingredients for variation.\nk: Disorder speed | Speed of that displacement when animated.\nuse: Quick fractal base height where no octave tuning is needed; add Histogram Scan afterwards.\ncop: @fractalnoise with a {Fractal Type} of Terrain and several {Max Octaves}; octave settings of the preset are not documented, so exact match is UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fractal-sum-2\nname: Fractal Sum 2\nsrc: /noises/fractal-sum-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Second preset variation of Fractal Sum Base with only Disorder controls exposed.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the noise ingredients for variation.\nk: Disorder speed | Speed of the displacement when animated.\nuse: Fractal base for rock, soil and generic breakup.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Dampened and adjusted {Roughness}; exact match UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fractal-sum-3\nname: Fractal Sum 3\nsrc: /noises/fractal-sum-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Third preset variation of Fractal Sum Base with only Disorder controls exposed.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the noise ingredients for variation.\nk: Disorder speed | Speed of the displacement when animated.\nuse: Fractal base for rock, soil and generic breakup.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Sharp for a more ridged feel; exact match UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fractal-sum-4\nname: Fractal Sum 4\nsrc: /noises/fractal-sum-4/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Fourth preset variation of Fractal Sum Base with only Disorder controls exposed.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the noise ingredients for variation.\nk: Disorder speed | Speed of the displacement when animated.\nuse: Fractal base for rock, soil and generic breakup.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Hybrid Terrain; exact match UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## bnw-spots-1\nname: BnW Spots 1\nsrc: /noises/bnw-spots-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: First of three rough black and white spot noises with Disorder controls and a Roughness balance of octaves.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Roughness | Balance of the noise octaves; higher makes high-frequency octaves more visible.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: High-contrast spot masks for pitting, corrosion starts and dirt, usually followed by Histogram Scan.\ncop: @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=Perlin, several {Max Octaves}) followed by @remap ({Operation}=Threshold) to get black and white spots; softness via {Width}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## bnw-spots-2\nname: BnW Spots 2\nsrc: /noises/bnw-spots-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Second rough black and white spot noise with the same controls as BnW Spots 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Same roles as BnW Spots 1.\ncop: Same chain as BnW Spots 1, with a different {Fractal Type} (Sharp) for a sharper spot edge; match UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## bnw-spots-3\nname: BnW Spots 3\nsrc: /noises/bnw-spots-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Third rough black and white spot noise with the same controls as BnW Spots 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Same roles as BnW Spots 1.\ncop: Same chain as BnW Spots 1 with a larger {Element Size}; match UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-1\nname: Dirt 1\nsrc: /noises/dirt-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: First of five grainy dirt noises sharing the Disorder controls; a rough speckled mask.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Grain and dirt masks to modulate roughness or to add micro relief to a height.\ncop: @grunge_layerednoise or @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=White (Random) mixed with Perlin) then @remap for contrast; the visual match is approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-2\nname: Dirt 2\nsrc: /noises/dirt-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Second grainy dirt noise with the same controls as Dirt 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nuse: Grain and dirt masks.\ncop: @grunge_layerednoise with different {Base Cells} and {Secondary Noise} sizes; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-3\nname: Dirt 3\nsrc: /noises/dirt-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Third grainy dirt noise with the same controls as Dirt 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nuse: Grain and dirt masks.\ncop: @grunge_layerednoise; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-4\nname: Dirt 4\nsrc: /noises/dirt-4/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Fourth grainy dirt noise with the same controls as Dirt 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nuse: Grain and dirt masks.\ncop: @grunge_layerednoise; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-5\nname: Dirt 5\nsrc: /noises/dirt-5/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Fifth grainy dirt noise with the same controls as Dirt 1.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nuse: Grain and dirt masks.\ncop: @grunge_layerednoise; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## dirt-gradient\nname: Dirt Gradient\nsrc: /noises/dirt-gradient/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Grainy dirt noise with a directional falloff gradient built in, so the dirt accumulates towards one side.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients (variation or animation).\nk: Disorder anisotropy | Narrows the direction of the Disorder displacement.\nk: Disorder anisotropy angle | Direction of that displacement.\nuse: Gravity-driven dirt buildup, such as grime collecting at the bottom of a wall, as a mask or shallow relief.\ncop: @ramp ({Type}=Vertical) multiplied with @fractalnoise grain through @blend, then @remap to set the falloff; or @grunge_moisture / @grunge_drips for gravity-led dirt.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## directional-noise-1\nname: Directional Noise 1\nsrc: /noises/directional-noise-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: First of four noises that stretch along a chosen direction (angle in turns) with optional random angle variation.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Angle | Direction of the noise, in turns from horizontal right.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle, in turns.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nk: Tile offset | Which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Brushed metal, wood grain, hair and streaky relief; feed into a height as long fine grooves.\ncop: @fractalnoise with unequal {Element Scale} (turn on Per-Component Controls) to stretch it, rotated with @xform2d; or @streakblur ({Angle}, {Length}) on @randommono for fine streaks.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## directional-noise-2\nname: Directional Noise 2\nsrc: /noises/directional-noise-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Second directional noise with the same angle and Disorder controls.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Angle | Direction of the noise, in turns.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle, in turns.\nuse: Same roles as Directional Noise 1 with a different grain character.\ncop: Same chain as Directional Noise 1 using another {Noise Type} (Worley Cellular F1 or White (Random)) before @streakblur; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## directional-noise-3\nname: Directional Noise 3\nsrc: /noises/directional-noise-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Third directional noise with the same angle and Disorder controls.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Angle | Direction of the noise, in turns.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle, in turns.\nuse: Same roles as Directional Noise 1.\ncop: Same chain as Directional Noise 1; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## directional-noise-4\nname: Directional Noise 4\nsrc: /noises/directional-noise-4/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Fourth directional noise with the same angle and Disorder controls.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Angle | Direction of the noise, in turns.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle, in turns.\nuse: Same roles as Directional Noise 1.\ncop: Same chain as Directional Noise 1; UNVERIFIED match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## directional-scratches\nname: Directional Scratches\nsrc: /noises/directional-scratches/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Random scattering of scratch strokes with adjustable angle, length and amount.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Angle | Direction of the scratches in turns.\nk: Angle random | Maximum random variation of the angle.\nk: Pattern amount | Multiplier for how many scratches are scattered.\nk: Pattern size | Bounding box of a scratch; the Y value sets the maximum length.\nk: Pattern size random | Random downscaling of scratches (Y affects the length).\nuse: Wear on metal and plastic; subtract thin scratches from a height or use them as masks for edge wear.\ncop: @shapescatter with a thin line stamp (@sdfshape line or @curve3d rasterised) for {Orient Ratio}/{Random Rotation} control, or @curvescatter; alternatively @streakblur applied to sparse @randommono dots.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fur-1\nname: Fur 1\nsrc: /noises/fur-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Straight fur-like noise made of fine parallel hairs.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect (1 to 8).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variation.\nuse: Short fur, grass blade relief and brushed surfaces at low amplitude.\ncop: @streakblur on @randommono (or high-frequency @fractalnoise) with a fixed {Angle}, then @equalize to restore contrast.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fur-2\nname: Fur 2\nsrc: /noises/fur-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Wavy fur-like noise whose hairs follow a sine-like wave.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect (1 to 8).\nk: Waves Scale | Size of the waves; bigger means fewer repeats.\nk: Waves Rotation | Extra twisting of the waves; keep low.\nuse: Wavy hair or grass height and groomed fabric pile.\ncop: @streakblur on noise with its direction layer ({Direction Type}) fed by a low-frequency @fractalnoise to bend the strands; approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fur-3\nname: Fur 3\nsrc: /noises/fur-3/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Spreading or bristle-type fur noise, strands fanning out.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect (1 to 8).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variation.\nk: Waves Amount | Number of waves in the spread.\nuse: Bristles, brush hairs or radiating strands in height maps.\ncop: @uvtopolar / @polartouv (radial remap) plus @streakblur, or @distort with a radial direction layer from @ramp ({Type}=Radial); approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## anisotropic-noise\nname: Anisotropic Noise\nsrc: /noises/anisotropic-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Horizontal or vertical stack of randomly valued strips fading into one another, with adjustable strip count and smoothness.\nk: X amount | Number of strips along X.\nk: Y amount | Number of strips along Y.\nk: Y amount by resolution | When true, the strip count along Y equals the pixel count.\nk: Rotate | Rotates the noise by 90 degrees.\nk: Smoothness | Fade between strips, 0 none and 1 across their whole length.\nk: Smoothness interpolation | Blend between linear (0) and Gaussian (1) interpolation of the fade.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients (variation or animation).\nuse: Brushed metal, wood grain streaks, sandpaper and any fine linear grain; the base for directional blurs and wood heights.\ncop: @randommono generated at low resolution in one axis (for example via @resample or {Element Scale} stretched by a factor on a white {Noise Type}) then smoothed with @blur ({Filter}=Gaussian); or @fractalnoise with a very large anisotropic {Element Scale}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## blue-noise-fast\nname: Blue Noise Fast\nsrc: /noises/blue-noise-fast/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Simple fast pixel-scale blue noise (evenly spread, no low-frequency clumping).\nk: Rotation | Rotates the internal calculation; the further from 1 the less regular the result.\nuse: Dithering, stochastic sampling thresholds and fine grain that avoids clumps.\ncop: @randommono with {Random per Pixel} on, passed through @filterfrequencies to suppress low frequencies (high-pass), then @equalize; or high-pass by subtracting a @blur of itself with @blend. Approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## white-noise\nname: White Noise\nsrc: /noises/white-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Per-pixel random values with a selectable histogram shape: uniform, Gaussian or triangle.\nk: Noise distribution | Shape of the value histogram (Uniform, Gaussian, Triangle).\nk: Disorder | Displaces the ingredients.\nuse: Seed for grain, input to blurs to build custom noises, dithering of 8-bit height banding.\ncop: @randommono with {Random per Pixel} on and {Seed}; Gaussian or triangle histograms are obtained by averaging two or more random layers with @average (Layer Merge) (UNVERIFIED), or by reshaping with @remap using a {Ramp}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## white-noise-fast\nname: White Noise Fast\nsrc: /noises/white-noise-fast/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Faster, lower quality version of White Noise for cases where quality matters less.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters for this node.\nuse: Same as White Noise when speed matters.\ncop: @randommono ({Random per Pixel}); the cost is already low in COP.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## waveform-1\nname: Waveform 1\nsrc: /noises/waveform-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Horizontal row of selectable patterns stacked into a waveform-like shape whose vertical extent follows noise, cosine or a custom function.\nk: Samples | Number of patterns placed along X to draw the waveform.\nk: Function | Function driving the vertical size of each sample (value noise, cosine, custom).\nk: Custom function | User-authored function giving the vertical size per sample.\nk: Roughness | Interpolates between a clean smooth waveform and a rougher one.\nk: Amplitude min. | Minimum thickness of the waveform.\nk: Amplitude max. | Maximum thickness of the waveform.\nk: Position | Whether the waveform is centered or sits at the bottom.\nk: Pattern | The pattern placed at each sample.\nuse: Audio-style or equalizer bars, stepped strata edges and ribbed relief.\ncop: @tilepattern columns with per-column height from @randommono or @wrangle (VEX) computing the bar height, drawn with {Draw As}; or @sdfshape per column via @shapescatter. Approximate and UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## caustics\nname: Caustics\nsrc: /noises/caustics/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Projects caustics from a height map and a single point light, with a colour variant that adds dispersion.\nk: Output Color Space | Raw or sRGB output.\nk: Photon Grid Size | Quality via photon grid resolution (Auto matches the input).\nk: Surface Height Scale | Multiplier for how the input height is interpreted.\nk: Surface Height Position | Distance of the refracting surface to the projection plane.\nk: Surface IOR | Refraction index (more colour dispersion in the colour version).\nk: Photon Size | Photon size, which affects crispness.\nk: Dispersion | Colour dispersion amount (colour version only).\nk: Jittering | Irregular jitter of the cast photons.\nk: Light Position | Position of the light (also a gizmo in the 2D view).\nuse: Underwater light patterns on pool floors or water-bed heights; take a water surface height as the input.\ncop: @heighttocaustics: {height} input is the refractive surface, {ground} input the receiving layer; set {Index of Refraction}, {Height Scale}, {Light Azimuth}/{Light Altitude} or {Light Position}, {Exposure}, {Photon Iterations}, {Denoise Iterations}.\nstatus: unverified\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5564,"cache_read_input_tokens":387937,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5564,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WTuLihgisNwRWsgQchZrfS":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/01a_noises.txt","content":"## perlin-noise\nname: Perlin Noise\nsrc: /noises/perlin-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Smooth, widely used gradient noise whose grayscale values vary continuously across the image, with a tileable grid controlled by Scale.\nk: Scale | Number of grid subdivisions per side, so higher values give smaller and denser features.\nk: Disorder | Moves the internal ingredients of the noise around, handy for variation or animation.\nk: Disorder speed | Distance travelled by the Disorder displacement per unit change, i.e. how fast it animates.\nk: Tile offset | Slides the visible window over the infinite noise plane to pick another crop of the same pattern.\nk: Non-square expansion | Keeps cells square on non-square canvases by extending the generation to the image bounds.\nuse: Soft base undulation for terrain, ground, plaster or clay heights; often warped or blended at low opacity to break flat areas before finer detail is added.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Perlin and {Fractal Type}=None for one octave; {Element Size} replaces Scale (size of one grid cell in image coordinates), {Offset} replaces Tile offset, {Tile Size} makes it repeat, and the Post Processing group ({Bias}, {Gain}, {Contrast}, {Clamp Minimum}, {Clamp Maximum}) shapes the histogram. Seeds are obtained by changing {Offset} or enabling {3D Noise} and moving the third coordinate (this plays the role of Disorder, UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## gaussian-noise\nname: Gaussian Noise\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-noise/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Smooth blobby noise built from overlapping gradients whose black to white transitions follow a bell curve, giving soft rounded lumps.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles; larger means more and smaller lumps.\nk: Disorder | Displaces the internal pieces of the noise for variation or animation.\nk: Disorder anisotropy | Narrows the directions in which Disorder pushes the pieces (0 means any direction).\nk: Disorder anisotropy angle | Direction of the Disorder displacement once anisotropy is non-zero.\nk: Tile offset | Chooses which crop of the infinite plane is rendered.\nuse: Go-to soft lumpy height for dunes, snow drifts, mud, cloth bulges and the large-scale pass of rock or ground before sharper noises are layered on top.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution (smooth value-style noise) and {Max Octaves} of 1 to 3 gives the same soft lumps; a cheaper alternative is @randommono blurred with @blur then stretched with @equalize. Use {Element Size} for Scale and {Tile Size} for seamless tiling.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## gaussian-spots-1\nname: Gaussian Spots 1\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-spots-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian-noise variant with narrower gradients, so the lumps read as separate soft spots instead of one continuous cloud.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision; more tiles give denser spots.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which part of the infinite plane is shown.\nuse: Pitting, soft pores, blotchy dirt masks or low-amplitude surface bumps on leather, plaster and skin heights.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution, then tighten the lumps with {Gain} above 0.5 or {Contrast} above 1 in Post Processing; or @cellularnoise with {Smooth Edge} and {Bevel} for round pebbles.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## gaussian-spots-2\nname: Gaussian Spots 2\nsrc: /noises/gaussian-spots-2/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Like Gaussian Spots 1 but with narrower gradients and higher frequencies, giving a finer sprinkling of soft spots.\nk: Scale | Grid subdivision of the noise tiles.\nk: Disorder | Displacement of the internal pieces (variation or animation).\nk: Tile offset | Which part of the infinite plane is shown.\nuse: Fine grain, micro pitting and small speckle heights that sit on top of a larger form.\ncop: Same chain as Gaussian Spots 1 with a smaller {Element Size} on @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution) and stronger {Contrast}; for hard speckle use @randommono blurred slightly and thresholded with @remap ({Operation}=Threshold).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## plasma\nname: Plasma\nsrc: /noises/plasma/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian-noise relative with longer dark streaks that act as valleys; the Scale control keeps the result tileable.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect (integer steps keep the tiling intact).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift of the noise for small variations.\nk: Non Square Expansion | Compensates squash and stretch on non-square images.\nuse: Veined, marbled or crackled low relief, canyon-like valleys in rock and ice heights.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Hybrid Terrain or Terrain and {Fold} (Post Processing) to create valley lines, optionally warped by @distort using a second noise as the direction layer. This is an approximation of the look (UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## liquid\nname: Liquid\nsrc: /noises/liquid/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Gaussian noise that is warped by itself, producing smooth swirling, liquid-looking undulations.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for variation.\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the self-warp that creates the flowing look.\nuse: Wet mud, lava crust, melted wax or flowing surface relief used as a base height or as a warp driver.\ncop: @fractalnoise ({Noise Type}=Sparse Convolution) feeding @distort twice: once as the source and once as the direction layer, with {Scale} on @distort acting as Warp Intensity.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fluid\nname: Fluid\nsrc: /noises/fluid/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: medium\npurpose: Generates a flowing or falling fluid pattern, useful where liquid runs under gravity such as rain streaks or leaks.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for variation.\nk: Warp Intensity | Intensity of the warp that bends the streaks.\nk: Pattern Size | Size of the drip or streak pattern.\nuse: Vertical rain, leaks and drip streaks as masks or very shallow relief on walls and metal.\ncop: @grunge_drips (drip pattern; {Density}, {Distortion}, {Primary Trailing}) or @grunge_leakingstreaks; plain streaks can also be made by @streakblur on sparse @randommono dots.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## microscope-view\nname: Microscope View\nsrc: /noises/microscope-view/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: low\npurpose: Warped noise reminiscent of bacteria or organisms seen under a microscope.\nk: Scale | Global scale of the effect.\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the warp; negative values are possible.\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variations.\nuse: Organic cell-like relief for alien skin, coral or moss-like micro heights.\ncop: @cellularnoise ({Smooth Edge} and {Bevel}) or @turingpatterns for organic blobs, then @distort with a smooth noise as the direction layer for the warp (approximate, UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## creased\nname: Creased\nsrc: /noises/creased/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Cloth-like creased heightmap with a semi-directional look and large-scale variation.\nk: Scale | Global scale (integer 1 to 8).\nk: Warp Intensity | Strength of the bend and warp that makes the creases (0 to 128).\nk: Disorder | Slightly offsets the layers used to build the noise (0 to 100).\nuse: Wrinkled fabric, crumpled paper or foil, leather folds, rock strata creases.\ncop: @fractalnoise with {Fractal Type}=Sharp and {Fold} in Post Processing for crease lines, then bend with @distort (direction layer from a low-frequency noise) to reproduce the Warp Intensity.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## crystal-1\nname: Crystal 1\nsrc: /noises/crystal-1/\ncat: Texture generators > Noises\nrel: high\npurpose: Worley/Voronoi-style noise with a slightly angular distance metric, useful for geometric and faceted patterns.\nk: Scale | Global scale (1 to 256).\nk: Disorder | Phase shift for small variations.\nuse: Crystals, faceted rock, ice shards, broken glass and hammered metal facets in height maps.\ncop: @crystalnoise (sharp, angular Worley type) with {Primary Metric}=Euclidean or Manhattan, {Element Size} for Scale and {Flatten Faces} to get flat facets; option