{"parentUuid":"ebbef98c-3078-455a-bebf-fdcce7e3dd61","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V5RwHUewrSjrKB8RPR6U1p","type":"tool_result","content":"#type:     node\r\n#context:  cop\r\n#internal: shapescatter\r\n#icon:     COP/shapescatter\r\n#since: 21.0\r\n\r\n= Scatter Shapes =\r\n\r\n\"\"\"Scatters input stamps across the image using randomization controls.\"\"\"\r\n\r\nThis node generates and scatters stamps (or multiple stamps) across the image. The node provides layers and parameters that let you further configure the randomization. When no `stamps` input is wired in, the node stamps a star pattern.\r\n\r\n[Image:/images/nodes/cop/shape_scatter.jpg]\r\n\r\nFor example, use this node to generate stamps in a grid formation or add jitter to create random scattering.\r\n\r\n@parameters\r\n\r\nSignature:\r\n    #id: signature\r\n    The layer type that the node outputs.\r\n\r\n    See [Signatures|/copernicus/glossary#signatures] for more information.\r\n\r\nLink Stamps:\r\n    #id: linkstamps\r\n    Makes the __Stamps X__ and __Stamps Y__ values equal.\r\n\r\nStamps X:\r\n    #id: stampsx\r\n    The amount of stamps to scatter along the X-axis of the image.\r\n    \r\nStamps Y:\r\n    #id: stampsy\r\n    The amount of stamps to scatter along the Y-axis of the image.\r\n\r\nStamps:\r\n    #id: stampsxy\r\n    The amount of stamps to scatter along both the X and Y-axes of the image.\r\n    \r\n== Scattering ==\r\n\r\nScatter Seed:\r\n    #id: seed\r\n    The seed value for all randomization that deals with stamp placement. This includes jitter, density, angle and direction, scale, and stretch.\r\n    \r\n=== Scale ===\r\n\r\nStamp Scaling:\r\n    #id: stampscaling\r\n    The way that the stamps scale in respect to the tile.\r\n\r\n    Image:\r\n        Makes all stamps conform to their original size relative to the image.\r\n\r\n    Stretch to Tile:\r\n        Makes all stamps squish or stretch to fit the tile.\r\n\r\nScale Layer Mask:\r\n    #id: scalemask\r\n    The amount of the `scale` input's mono value to mix in with the __Image Scale__ or __Tile Scale__ value, which impacts the stamps' scale. Higher values mix in more of the input's value. Lower values mix in less of the input's value.\r\n\r\nScale Mode:\r\n    #id: scalemode\r\n    The method of post-processing to apply to the scale of stamps.\r\n\r\n    Uniform:\r\n        No post-processing.\r\n\r\n    Variance:\r\n        Apply random variance to the scale.\r\n\r\n    Min/Max:\r\n        The minimum and maximum range of values that each stamp randomly picks from, and then multiplies against the original __Image Scale__ or __Tile Scale__.\r\n\r\nImage Scale:\r\n    #id: scale\r\n    Sets the scale of the stamps in [image space|/copernicus/spaces].\r\n\r\nTile Scale:\r\n    #id: scale_tile\r\n    Sets the scale of the stamps in tile space, where a value of `1` is one tile space and a value of `2` is two times a tile space.\r\n\r\nScale Variance:\r\n    #id: scalevariance\r\n    The amount of random variance to apply to the scale. This is applied as a post-process.\r\n    \r\n    If __Tile Scale__ is `1` and __Scale Variance__ `1` for example, this represents a 100% range in sizes. This means the output stamps have a random scale from `0.5 * Scale` to `2.0 * Scale`.\r\n\r\nMin Value:\r\n    #id: minscale\r\n    The minimum value that the stamps can randomly select, and then multiply against the original __Image Scale__ or __Tile Scale__. This is applied as a post-process.\r\n\r\nMax Value:\r\n    #id: maxscale\r\n    The maximum value that the stamps can randomly select, and then multiply against the original __Image Scale__ or __Tile Scale__. This is applied as a post-process.\r\n    \r\n=== Jitter ===\r\n\r\nJitter Layer Mask:\r\n    #id: jittermask\r\n    The amount of the `jitter` input's UV value to mix in with the __Jitter__ value, which impacts the random position offset. Higher values mix in more of the input's value. Lower values mix in less of the input's value.\r\n    \r\nJitter Layer Strength:\r\n    #id: relaxstrength\r\n    The amount by which to multiply the  `jitter` input's UV value.\r\n    \r\nPost-Jitter:\r\n    #id: applyjitterlate\r\n    Sample the input layers such as `scale`, `angle`, using the stamps' position post-jitter. \r\n\r\nJitter:\r\n    #id: jitter\r\n    The amount of random position offset to add to each point's position on the grid.\r\n    \r\nJitter Scale:\r\n    #id: jitterscalex, jitterscaley\r\n    When the [Smallicon:BUTTONS/decompose_transforms] __Per-Component Controls__ button is on, this is the per-axis scaling of the random position offset for the created stamps.\r\n    \r\n=== Angle ===\r\n220:Blend Method:\r\n221-    #id: blend\r\n222-    When mulitple stamps overlap, this controls how they blend together.\r\n223-\r\n224-    Depth-Sorted Over:\r\n225-        Post-sort the points by their depth. The farthest points are rendered first, so closer points paint over them. Since this has expensive internal operations, there's a performance loss. This is also unforgiving to partially transparent sta\n226-\r\n227-        NOTE:\r\n228-            When you wire in a `sort` input, that value is used instead.\r\n229-\r\n230-    Unsorted Depth-Weighted Over:\r\n231-        Apply a non-linear weighting that approximates depth-sorting without the need for sorting.\r\n232-\r\n233-        NOTE:\r\n234-            When you wire in a `sort` input, that value is used instead.\r\n235-\r\n236-    Add:\r\n237-        Add the overlapping pixel values of the stamps together.\r\n238-\r\n239-    Subtract:\r\n240-        Subtract the original pixel value from the new overlapping stamp's pixel value. This method works better when you're not subtracting from zero.\r\n241-\r\n242-    Multiply:","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"30db71cf-c475-43ea-aab4-02f386610417","timestamp":"2026-10-1