{"parentUuid":"54f1f8df-cf35-490e-ac2a-e15b94c147b6","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvctKsdm8e2BgRgod2nN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BmgfvMwJmEMH3bqBw3aqsv","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/03_adjustments.txt","content":"## histogram-scan\nname: Histogram Scan\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-scan/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Intuitive remap of the brightness and contrast of a grayscale input; it grows or shrinks masks by moving a threshold position and sets how hard the transition is.\nk: Position | Moves the midpoint of the result (a brightness-like control); on a gradient it expands or shrinks the transition. The documentation warns that 0 gives an all-black result, so start near 0.5.\nk: Contrast | Sets how hard the transition is, from soft ramp to hard threshold.\nk: Invert Position | Inverts the final result.\nuse: The most used height node: turn noise into stone shapes or cracks, grow or shrink masks, set the level where a pattern starts to show; a hard Contrast gives binary masks for Flood Fill and Edge Detect.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Threshold (SideFX lists Histogram nodes under Remap and says to use Threshold like Histogram Scan). {Threshold} plays the role of Position (its direction is inverted: Threshold about 1 minus Position, UNVERIFIED), {Width} is the softness (Contrast 1 corresponds to Width 0, lower Contrast widens it, relation UNVERIFIED), {Threshold When}=Greater, {Output Min}=0 and {Output Max}=1; the invert toggle is a swap of {Output Min} and {Output Max} or @invert.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## histogram-scan-non-uniform\nname: Histogram Scan Non-Uniform\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-scan-non-uniform/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Histogram Scan whose position and contrast can be driven per pixel by input maps instead of one value for the whole image.\nk: Use Position Input | Enables the Position Map input.\nk: position | Controls or modifies the map results driving the position.\nk: Use Contrast Input | Enables the Contrast Map input.\nk: contrast | Controls or modifies the map results driving the contrast.\nuse: Locally varying thresholds, such as eroding a mask more on one side or letting a gradient decide where a pattern appears.\ncop: Per-pixel threshold with @compare ({Falloff}=Linear, {Tolerance} acting as softness, inputs {i:A} and {i:B} for source and position map) or @compareblend; @remap only has per-pixel inputs for the input and output min and max ({i:input_min}, {i:input_max}, {i:output_min}, {i:output_max}), so a position map can drive {i:input_min} and {i:input_max} in the Remap operation. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-select\nname: Histogram Select\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-select/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Selects a band of grayscale values around a position, fading out over a given range, with adjustable contrast of the falloff.\nk: Position | Centre of the selected value band.\nk: Range | Width of the selected band.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the falloff at the band edges.\nuse: Isolating mid-height strata, contour bands or terrace edges from a height map.\ncop: @remap with {Use Ramp} on and a bump-shaped mono {Ramp} (peak at Position, base width Range, steepness set by interpolation); or two @remap threshold passes (greater than lower edge, less than upper edge) merged with @blend {Mode}=Minimum. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-range\nname: Histogram Range\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-range/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Reduces and moves the range of a grayscale input, much like moving the Levels min and max sliders inwards.\nk: Range | How much the range is reduced.\nk: Position | Offset of the reduced range, i.e. its midpoint.\nuse: Compressing a height into a narrow band (for example 0.4 to 0.6) before adding to a base height, or limiting amplitude of a detail layer.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Remap: {Output Min} and {Output Max} define the reduced band, derived from Range and Position (band width equal to Range, centred by Position, UNVERIFIED), keeping {Input Min}=0 and {Input Max}=1.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-shift\nname: Histogram Shift\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-shift/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Shifts the whole value range and wraps around at the limits, so 1.0 equals a full rotation.\nk: Position | Amount of shift (0 and 1 are identical).\nuse: Rotating the grey levels of a noise to move its peaks (cyclic remap), creating alternative bands from a gradient.\ncop: Add the offset with @bright ({Shift}) or @blend ({Mode}=Add) with a @constant, then wrap with @remap using {Outside Range}=Repeat, or @function with {Function Class}=Arithmetic and {Arithmetic}=Modulus. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-compute\nname: Histogram Compute\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-compute/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Computes the histogram of a grayscale image as a row of pixels (and a cumulative distribution), where each pixel holds the population of its grey value.\nk: Histogram resolution | Width of the histogram row (finer value distribution when larger).\nuse: Building custom equalisation, adaptive thresholds and analysis of height distributions.\ncop: @histogram ({Buckets}, {Minimum}, {Maximum}, {Mode}, outputs {o:histogram} and {o:counts}); the cumulative distribution is obtained with @prefixsum ({Operation}=Add).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-equalize\nname: Histogram Equalize\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-equalize/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Redistributes grayscale values so the histogram becomes as flat as possible.\nk: Histogram resolution | Number of histogram bins.\nk: Histogram smoothing | Smooths the distribution by redistributing values.\nuse: Making a murky height use the whole range evenly, or getting a uniform distribution before thresholding.\ncop: Cumulative distribution as @histogram then @prefixsum, then remap the image through that curve (for example by sampling the CDF with @sample or an @opencl pass). The COP @equalize node only stretches or shifts the range and is NOT a true histogram equalisation. UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-render\nname: Histogram Render\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-render/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Draws the histogram of a grayscale image as a picture.\nk: Histogram resolution | Width of the histogram.\nk: Automatic scale | Rescales the histogram to the full image height.\nk: Scale | Vertical scale of the histogram.\nk: Flip Y axis | Mirrors the histogram vertically.\nuse: Checking how a height is distributed before and after remapping.\ncop: @histogram with {Mode}=Graph (or Colored Bars / Separate Bars), {Buckets}, {Scale}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## auto-levels\nname: Auto Levels\nsrc: /adjustments/auto-levels/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Stretches the input so its darkest value becomes black and its brightest white, maximising contrast.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Normalising a noise or blurred height back to the 0 to 1 range after warps and blurs; used constantly in height graphs.\ncop: @equalize with {Equalize By}=Stretch to Black and White (SideFX-confirmed correspondence); {Fit Method} chooses Shifting or Scaling values; {Black} and {White} set the target range.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## min-max\nname: Min Max\nsrc: /adjustments/min-max/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Finds the lowest and highest values of a grayscale input and returns them as values, as a manual alternative to Auto Levels (feed them into a Levels node through value inputs).\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Exact normalisation of a height, or driving thresholds from the real value range.\ncop: @statistics (SideFX-confirmed) with outputs {o:minimum}, {o:maximum} and {o:average}; then @remap with {Input Min} and {Input Max} set by the {Compute Range} button or driven by the {i:input_min} and {i:input_max} inputs.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## threshold\nname: Threshold\nsrc: /adjustments/threshold/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Returns white where the chosen comparison between the pixel value and a threshold holds; like Histogram Scan at full contrast but cheaper and more exact.\nk: Threshold | Luminance value the pixel is compared against.\nk: Mode | Comparison criterion (greater, greater or equal, lower, lower or equal).\nuse: Binary masks of cracks, plateaus or flat areas from a height or gradient, before Distance or Flood Fill.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Threshold, {Width}=0, {Threshold When}=Greater, Greater or Equal, Less or Less or Equal (these four options match the SD modes one to one); alternatively @compare with {True If} and {Comparison Value}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## quantize-grayscale\nname: Quantize Grayscale\nsrc: /adjustments/quantize-grayscale/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Approximates a grayscale range with a given number of steps, optionally with sloped transitions between the steps, producing terraces. (The documentation description text is a copy error about splines; this entry follows the parameter table.)\nk: Steps | Number of distinct values.\nk: Offset | Shifts the results along the range.\nk: Slope | Adds a slope between steps, up to the full span of a step.\nk: Slope Curve | Shape of that slope: linear, smoothstep, or a curve input.\nuse: Terraced hills, stepped rock strata, banded plateaus and posterised masks; the Slope parameter makes beveled step edges.\ncop: @quantize with {Method}=Number of Segments, {Segments}, {Offset} and {Rounding}=Floor; the soft riser (Slope) is made by blurring slightly with @blur or by using @heightfield_terrace on a heightfield version of the layer (its stepped plains with sloped transitions). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## quantize\nname: Quantize\nsrc: /adjustments/quantize/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Approximates each channel of a colour input to a chosen number of values, which introduces banding. (Documentation page title: Quantize Color (Simple).)\nk: Quantize Red Channel | Number of steps for red.\nk: Quantize Green Channel | Number of steps for green.\nk: Quantize Blue Channel | Number of steps for blue.\nk: Quantize Alpha Channel | Number of steps for alpha.\nuse: Posterised colour looks and stepped normal or colour IDs.\ncop: @quantize ({Method}=Number of Segments, {Segments}) applied to the RGB layer (the same steps for all channels; per-channel steps need @channelsplit and @channeljoin).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## quantize-color\nname: Quantize Color\nsrc: /adjustments/quantize-color/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Reduces an image to a limited number of colours with dithering, and also outputs an ID map of the flattened areas and the palette.\nk: Max. color amount | Maximum number of colours in the result.\nk: Contour smoothing | Radius of a pre-smoothing used to simplify the result.\nk: Dithering | Amount of dithering used to recreate gradients.\nk: Dithering pattern | Dither pattern type.\nk: Ignore alpha | Ignores the alpha channel when picking areas.\nuse: Stylised colour flattening and extracting a palette plus ID map from a photo.\ncop: No single node. @quantize plus @segmentbyvalue for IDs, or @kuwaharafilter for painterly flattening; the palette extraction needs a custom chain (UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## clamp\nname: Clamp\nsrc: /adjustments/clamp/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Limits values to a lower and upper bound.\nk: Min | Lower clamp limit.\nk: Max | Upper clamp limit.\nk: Apply to Alpha | Whether the alpha channel is clamped too.\nuse: Cutting off over-range values after blends and additions to keep a valid 0 to 1 height; flattening peaks.\ncop: @clamp with {Lower Limit} and {Upper Limit}; {Clamped Values}=Replace with Limit Value is the plain clamp (Soft Clip is an extra option).\ngray: Grayscale-only clamp.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## invert\nname: Invert\nsrc: /adjustments/invert/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Inverts the colours of the input; the grayscale node is the one to use for grayscale data.\nk: Invert | Switches the effect on or off.\nuse: Flipping a height to turn bumps into dents, or a mask into its complement.\ncop: @invert with {Invert By}=Complement (1 minus x); alternatively the {Complement} post-process on noise nodes.\ngray: Use for grayscale inputs.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pow\nname: Pow\nsrc: /adjustments/pow/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Raises the input to an exponent, similar to changing the Levels midpoint but as an exact mathematical power.\nk: exponent | Exponent applied to the values (0 to 10).\nuse: Reshaping height profiles: exponent above 1 flattens low areas and sharpens peaks, below 1 does the opposite; shaping mask falloffs.\ncop: @gamma ({Gamma}, with {Invert} to flip its direction) or @function with {Function Class}=Powers and {Power}=pow(x, E) and {Exponent}.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## contrast-luminosity\nname: Contrast/Luminosity\nsrc: /adjustments/contrast-luminosity/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Simple contrast and luminosity (brightness) adjustment.\nk: Contrast | Contrast of the result.\nk: Luminosity | Brightness of the result.\nuse: Quick tuning of a mask or height without a Levels node.\ncop: @contrast ({Contrast}, {Contrast Center}) followed by @bright ({Brightness}).\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## levels-note-height\nname: Auto Levels Note\nsrc: /adjustments/auto-levels/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Placeholder removed.\nuse: n/a\ncop: n/a\nstatus: unverified\n\n## highpass\nname: Highpass\nsrc: /adjustments/highpass/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Removes large-scale luminance differences, keeping only the details; typically used to clean photos for tiling or to isolate fine relief from a height.\nk: Radius | Filter radius; a small radius removes small differences, a large one removes large areas.\nuse: Splitting a height into large-scale shape and small-scale detail, flattening gradients in scanned heights before Make It Tile.\ncop: @blur copy subtracted from the original with @blend ({Mode}=Subtract), then add mid-grey with @bright ({Shift}) so zero detail sits at 0.5; or @filterfrequencies to remove low frequencies. The exact offset convention is UNVERIFIED.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## luminance-highpass\nname: Luminance Highpass\nsrc: /adjustments/luminance-highpass/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Performs the highpass on the luminance only, which cancels baked-in lighting while preserving colour better.\nk: Radius | Radius of the highpass; a smaller radius cancels smaller lighting.\nuse: De-lighting photographed textures before extracting a height.\ncop: @hsv with {Operation} on the value channel or @mono (luminance) split, highpass the luminance as in Highpass, then recombine with @hsv (UNVERIFIED chain).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## lighting-cancel-high-frequencies\nname: Lighting Cancel High Frequencies\nsrc: /adjustments/lighting-cancel-high-frequencies/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Cancels small, high-frequency lighting details in colour images without desaturating as much as Highpass.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the cancellation.\nk: Radius | Size of the lighting details cancelled (0 to 10).\nuse: Removing tiny shading from scans before using them as albedo.\ncop: @blur at a small radius blended with the original through @blend and {Mask}; or @filterfrequencies (suppress high frequencies). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## lighting-cancel-low-frequencies\nname: Lighting Cancel Low Frequencies\nsrc: /adjustments/lighting-cancel-low-frequencies/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Highpass-like removal of large lighting gradients that keeps the colour saturation.\nk: Cancellation Radius | Radius of the highpass effect.\nuse: Flattening uneven lighting on photo scans and on low-frequency height drift.\ncop: Subtract a heavily blurred copy (@blur with a large {Size}) via @blend {Mode}=Subtract then re-centre with @bright; or @filterfrequencies. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## height-map-frequencies-mapper\nname: Height Map Frequencies Mapper\nsrc: /adjustments/height-map-frequencies/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Splits a height map into two maps, one holding the large-scale differences and one holding the small-scale ones.\nk: Relief | Controls the detail size of the displacement output.\nuse: Separating macro form from micro detail so they can be processed, scaled or re-blended independently in a height graph.\ncop: Low frequency = @blur of the height; high frequency = height minus low frequency with @blend {Mode}=Subtract (offset by mid-grey if a signed result must be stored as unsigned); @filterfrequencies can do the split in frequency space. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## channel-mixer\nname: Channel Mixer\nsrc: /adjustments/channel-mixer/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Mixes, swaps and blends the RGB channels, useful for swizzling, accurate grayscale conversion and channel packing.\nk: Red Channel | Contribution of input RGB channels to the output red (percent, -200 to 200).\nk: Green Channel | Contribution to output green.\nk: Blue Channel | Contribution to output blue.\nk: Monochrome | Outputs a single mono channel for precise grayscale conversion.\nuse: Packing height, roughness and AO into one texture, or extracting a custom grayscale from a colour scan.\ncop: @mono with {Operation}=Custom and {Weights} for the Monochrome mode; swizzles and packing with @channelsplit, @channelswap and @channeljoin; arbitrary mixing matrices with @wrangle.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## chrominance-extract\nname: Chrominance Extract\nsrc: /adjustments/chrominance-extract/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Extracts the chrominance of the input, stripping the luminance.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Separating colour information from lighting in scans.\ncop: Colour minus its luminance: @mono (luminance) subtracted from the RGB layer with @blend {Mode}=Subtract, or @hsv with {Value Scale} set to a constant. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## color-match\nname: Color Match\nsrc: /adjustments/color-match/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Matches a source colour range (averaged, set, or taken from an input) to a target colour range, with optional variation and mask output.\nk: Source Color Mode | Source colour from the average of the input, a parameter, or an input.\nk: Source Color | Source colour when the mode is Parameter.\nk: Target Color Mode | Target colour from a parameter or an image.\nk: Target Color | Target colour when the mode is Parameter.\nk: Custom Color Variation | Adds extra colour variation.\nk: Use Mask | Toggles mask input or output.\nuse: Colour grading photo scans to a reference; irrelevant for pure heights.\ncop: @colorcorrect (shadow, midtone, highlight add and multiply) driven by values from @statistics ({o:average}); @hsv for hue matching. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## color-to-mask\nname: Color To Mask\nsrc: /adjustments/color-to-mask/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Extracts a grayscale mask from selected colours of a colour image.\nk: Use color input | Uses an input image instead of a uniform reference colour.\nk: Color | Reference colour.\nk: Threshold | Distance to the reference below which colours are selected.\nk: Selection fade | Fades the selection with distance.\nk: Distance color space | Colour space used for the distance.\nuse: Masks from material ID maps or painted colours.\ncop: @chromakey (hue, saturation and luminance ranges; {Threshold}, {Width}, outputs {o:matte}) or @compare of an ID colour layer.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## convert-to-linear\nname: Convert To Linear\nsrc: /adjustments/convert-to-linear/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Converts an sRGB image to linear colour space, useful for photo sources.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Linearising scanned albedo before processing; not applied to height data.\ncop: @ociotransform with {Transform Method}=Between Spaces ({Original Space} sRGB, {To Space} linear), or @gamma with an exponent of about 2.2 (approximation).\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## convert-to-srgb\nname: Convert to sRGB\nsrc: /adjustments/convert-to-srgb/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Converts a linear image to sRGB colour space.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Preparing linear data for display or export as 8-bit colour.\ncop: @ociotransform ({Original Space} linear, {To Space} sRGB) or @gamma with the inverse exponent (approximation).\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## grayscale-conversion-advanced\nname: Grayscale Conversion Advanced\nsrc: /adjustments/grayscale-conversion-adv/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Quick grayscale conversion with presets: desaturation, luma, average, maximum and minimum.\nk: Grayscale Type | Conversion method.\nuse: Extracting a height or mask from a coloured scan or from baked colour data.\ncop: @mono with {Operation} = SGI/NTSC/HDTV Luminance (luma), Average, Maximum Channel or Minimum Channel; desaturation corresponds to Value or Saturation operations of @hsv.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## hald-clut\nname: Hald CLUT\nsrc: /adjustments/hald-clut/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Applies a LUT in Hald format (4096 by 4096) to the input image.\nk: LUT Intensity by Alpha | Weights the LUT effect by the alpha channel.\nuse: Colour grading; not used for heights.\ncop: @ociotransform if the LUT is wrapped in an OCIO configuration; otherwise sample the Hald image with @sample through a UV layer computed from the input colour (@wrangle). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## hdr-range-viewer\nname: HDR Range Viewer\nsrc: /adjustments/hdr-range-viewer/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Debug tool that highlights areas outside the 0 to 1 range.\nk: Range Min | Lower bound where highlighting starts (-2 to 0).\nk: Range Max | Upper bound highlighted up to (1 to 3).\nuse: Verifying that heights stay inside the valid range after additive blends.\ncop: @compare or @compareblend against {Comparison Value} 0 and 1, tinted with @constant; or read @statistics for the extremes.\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## id-to-mask\nname: ID To Mask Grayscale\nsrc: /adjustments/id-to-mask/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Builds a mask from an ID map, making white the pixels whose ID matches a selection (solo or range).\nk: Selection mode | Select a single ID or a range.\nk: ID Integer | The ID value turned white.\nk: ID Range | Start and end of the selected ID range.\nuse: Isolating single stones or tiles after Flood Fill or Tile Sampler IDs.\ncop: @idtomask ({Keep by Range} with {Start ID} and {End ID}, {Invert Mask}); the ID layer comes from outputs such as {o:id} of @tilepattern or @segmentbyconnectivity.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## replace-color\nname: Replace Color\nsrc: /adjustments/replace-color/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Hue shifts a source colour towards a target colour.\nk: Source Color | Colour to start shifting hue from.\nk: Target Color | Colour to shift the hue towards.\nuse: Recolouring ID maps and albedo; not for heights.\ncop: @hsv with {Operation}=Hue/Sat/Val Adjust and {Hue Shift} set to the hue difference between the two colours, restricted by {Mask} from @chromakey.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## replace-color-range\nname: Replace Color Range\nsrc: /adjustments/replace-color-range/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Replaces a source colour by a target colour with tolerance range and falloff controls.\nk: Source Color | Colour to replace.\nk: Target Color | Colour to replace it with.\nk: Source Range | Tolerance of the picked source colour.\nk: Threshold | Falloff or contrast of the range.\nuse: Re-colouring parts of a baked material ID map.\ncop: @chromakey (matte output) used as the {mask} of a @blend between the original and a @constant target colour.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## apply-color-palette\nname: Apply Color Palette\nsrc: /adjustments/apply-color-palette/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Applies the colours of an ordered palette to an image through an ID map (palette index equals ID value).\nuse: Colouring flat ID maps (stones, tiles) with a palette.\ncop: @sample (UV Sample) of a palette strip using the ID divided by the colour count as U, or per-ID colours with @randomrgb driven by an ID layer. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## create-color-palette-16\nname: Create Color Palette (16)\nsrc: /adjustments/create-color-palette-16/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Builds an ordered list of up to 16 colours output as a palette, appending to an existing palette if connected.\nk: Color amount | Number of colours added.\nuse: Palette authoring for ID-based colouring.\ncop: A colour strip built with @contactsheet from @constant layers, or a @ramp with constant interpolation. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## modify-color-palette\nname: Modify Color Palette\nsrc: /adjustments/modify-color-palette/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Modifies chosen colours of a palette (override or HSL offset) and applies it to an image through an ID map.\nk: Color selection mode | How the target palette colour is chosen.\nk: Color index | Index of the target colour.\nk: Mode | Override colour or HSL offset.\nk: Opacity | Blend between original and modified colour.\nk: Override color | Replacement colour.\nk: HSL | Hue, saturation and lightness offsets.\nuse: Tweaking ID colours without rebuilding the palette.\ncop: @hsv on the palette strip, masked by @idtomask for the targeted index; UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## view-color-palette\nname: View Color Palette\nsrc: /adjustments/view-color-palette/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Packs a palette into a square or rectangle for easier viewing.\nuse: Visual check of a palette.\ncop: @contactsheet of the palette colours.\nstatus: unverified\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3230,"cache_read_input_tokens":552753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3230,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01BmgfvMwJmEMH3bqBw3aqsv":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/03_adjustments.txt","content":"## histogram-scan\nname: Histogram Scan\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-scan/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Intuitive remap of the brightness and contrast of a grayscale input; it grows or shrinks masks by moving a threshold position and sets how hard the transition is.\nk: Position | Moves the midpoint of the result (a brightness-like control); on a gradient it expands or shrinks the transition. The documentation warns that 0 gives an all-black result, so start near 0.5.\nk: Contrast | Sets how hard the transition is, from soft ramp to hard threshold.\nk: Invert Position | Inverts the final result.\nuse: The most used height node: turn noise into stone shapes or cracks, grow or shrink masks, set the level where a pattern starts to show; a hard Contrast gives binary masks for Flood Fill and Edge Detect.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Threshold (SideFX lists Histogram nodes under Remap and says to use Threshold like Histogram Scan). {Threshold} plays the role of Position (its direction is inverted: Threshold about 1 minus Position, UNVERIFIED), {Width} is the softness (Contrast 1 corresponds to Width 0, lower Contrast widens it, relation UNVERIFIED), {Threshold When}=Greater, {Output Min}=0 and {Output Max}=1; the invert toggle is a swap of {Output Min} and {Output Max} or @invert.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## histogram-scan-non-uniform\nname: Histogram Scan Non-Uniform\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-scan-non-uniform/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Histogram Scan whose position and contrast can be driven per pixel by input maps instead of one value for the whole image.\nk: Use Position Input | Enables the Position Map input.\nk: position | Controls or modifies the map results driving the position.\nk: Use Contrast Input | Enables the Contrast Map input.\nk: contrast | Controls or modifies the map results driving the contrast.\nuse: Locally varying thresholds, such as eroding a mask more on one side or letting a gradient decide where a pattern appears.\ncop: Per-pixel threshold with @compare ({Falloff}=Linear, {Tolerance} acting as softness, inputs {i:A} and {i:B} for source and position map) or @compareblend; @remap only has per-pixel inputs for the input and output min and max ({i:input_min}, {i:input_max}, {i:output_min}, {i:output_max}), so a position map can drive {i:input_min} and {i:input_max} in the Remap operation. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-select\nname: Histogram Select\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-select/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Selects a band of grayscale values around a position, fading out over a given range, with adjustable contrast of the falloff.\nk: Position | Centre of the selected value band.\nk: Range | Width of the selected band.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the falloff at the band edges.\nuse: Isolating mid-height strata, contour bands or terrace edges from a height map.\ncop: @remap with {Use Ramp} on and a bump-shaped mono {Ramp} (peak at Position, base width Range, steepness set by interpolation); or two @remap threshold passes (greater than lower edge, less than upper edge) merged with @blend {Mode}=Minimum. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-range\nname: Histogram Range\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-range/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Reduces and moves the range of a grayscale input, much like moving the Levels min and max sliders inwards.\nk: Range | How much the range is reduced.\nk: Position | Offset of the reduced range, i.e. its midpoint.\nuse: Compressing a height into a narrow band (for example 0.4 to 0.6) before adding to a base height, or limiting amplitude of a detail layer.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Remap: {Output Min} and {Output Max} define the reduced band, derived from Range and Position (band width equal to Range, centred by Position, UNVERIFIED), keeping {Input Min}=0 and {Input Max}=1.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-shift\nname: Histogram Shift\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-shift/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Shifts the whole value range and wraps around at the limits, so 1.0 equals a full rotation.\nk: Position | Amount of shift (0 and 1 are identical).\nuse: Rotating the grey levels of a noise to move its peaks (cyclic remap), creating alternative bands from a gradient.\ncop: Add the offset with @bright ({Shift}) or @blend ({Mode}=Add) with a @constant, then wrap with @remap using {Outside Range}=Repeat, or @function with {Function Class}=Arithmetic and {Arithmetic}=Modulus. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-compute\nname: Histogram Compute\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-compute/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Computes the histogram of a grayscale image as a row of pixels (and a cumulative distribution), where each pixel holds the population of its grey value.\nk: Histogram resolution | Width of the histogram row (finer value distribution when larger).\nuse: Building custom equalisation, adaptive thresholds and analysis of height distributions.\ncop: @histogram ({Buckets}, {Minimum}, {Maximum}, {Mode}, outputs {o:histogram} and {o:counts}); the cumulative distribution is obtained with @prefixsum ({Operation}=Add).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-equalize\nname: Histogram Equalize\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-equalize/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Redistributes grayscale values so the histogram becomes as flat as possible.\nk: Histogram resolution | Number of histogram bins.\nk: Histogram smoothing | Smooths the distribution by redistributing values.\nuse: Making a murky height use the whole range evenly, or getting a uniform distribution before thresholding.\ncop: Cumulative distribution as @histogram then @prefixsum, then remap the image through that curve (for example by sampling the CDF with @sample or an @opencl pass). The COP @equalize node only stretches or shifts the range and is NOT a true histogram equalisation. UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## histogram-render\nname: Histogram Render\nsrc: /adjustments/histogram-render/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Draws the histogram of a grayscale image as a picture.\nk: Histogram resolution | Width of the histogram.\nk: Automatic scale | Rescales the histogram to the full image height.\nk: Scale | Vertical scale of the histogram.\nk: Flip Y axis | Mirrors the histogram vertically.\nuse: Checking how a height is distributed before and after remapping.\ncop: @histogram with {Mode}=Graph (or Colored Bars / Separate Bars), {Buckets}, {Scale}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## auto-levels\nname: Auto Levels\nsrc: /adjustments/auto-levels/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Stretches the input so its darkest value becomes black and its brightest white, maximising contrast.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Normalising a noise or blurred height back to the 0 to 1 range after warps and blurs; used constantly in height graphs.\ncop: @equalize with {Equalize By}=Stretch to Black and White (SideFX-confirmed correspondence); {Fit Method} chooses Shifting or Scaling values; {Black} and {White} set the target range.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## min-max\nname: Min Max\nsrc: /adjustments/min-max/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: medium\npurpose: Finds the lowest and highest values of a grayscale input and returns them as values, as a manual alternative to Auto Levels (feed them into a Levels node through value inputs).\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Exact normalisation of a height, or driving thresholds from the real value range.\ncop: @statistics (SideFX-confirmed) with outputs {o:minimum}, {o:maximum} and {o:average}; then @remap with {Input Min} and {Input Max} set by the {Compute Range} button or driven by the {i:input_min} and {i:input_max} inputs.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## threshold\nname: Threshold\nsrc: /adjustments/threshold/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Returns white where the chosen comparison between the pixel value and a threshold holds; like Histogram Scan at full contrast but cheaper and more exact.\nk: Threshold | Luminance value the pixel is compared against.\nk: Mode | Comparison criterion (greater, greater or equal, lower, lower or equal).\nuse: Binary masks of cracks, plateaus or flat areas from a height or gradient, before Distance or Flood Fill.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Threshold, {Width}=0, {Threshold When}=Greater, Greater or Equal, Less or Less or Equal (these four options match the SD modes one to one); alternatively @compare with {True If} and {Comparison Value}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## quantize-grayscale\nname: Quantize Grayscale\nsrc: /adjustments/quantize-grayscale/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: high\npurpose: Approximates a grayscale range with a given number of steps, optionally with sloped transitions between the steps, producing terraces. (The documentation description text is a copy error about splines; this entry follows the parameter table.)\nk: Steps | Number of distinct values.\nk: Offset | Shifts the results along the range.\nk: Slope | Adds a slope between steps, up to the full span of a step.\nk: Slope Curve | Shape of that slope: linear, smoothstep, or a curve input.\nuse: Terraced hills, stepped rock strata, banded plateaus and posterised masks; the Slope parameter makes beveled step edges.\ncop: @quantize with {Method}=Number of Segments, {Segments}, {Offset} and {Rounding}=Floor; the soft riser (Slope) is made by blurring slightly with @blur or by using @heightfield_terrace on a heightfield version of the layer (its stepped plains with sloped transitions). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## quantize\nname: Quantize\nsrc: /adjustments/quantize/\ncat: Texture filters > Adjustments\nrel: low\npurpose: Approximates each channel of a colour input to a chosen number of values, which introduces banding. (Documentation page title: Quantize Color (Simple).)\nk: Quantize Red Channel | Number of steps for red.\nk: Quantize Green Channel | Number of steps for green.\nk: Quantize Blue Channel | Number of steps for blue.\nk: Quantize Alpha Channel | Number of steps for alpha.\nuse: Posterised colour looks and stepped normal or colour IDs.\