{"parentUuid":"c7dabd5b-4e41-47e4-9751-475cc1424a28","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017oXk7j7X3pCZe7r9cK1WV6","type":"tool_result","content":"#nodes 10\n=== Anisotropic Blur  [filters/blurs/anisotropic-blur]  (Filters > Blurs)\nD: Performs a high quality directional blur, with a few settings to customise appearance. Also known as \"motion blur\". Important: make sure to use the appropriate version for your input! Use \"Anisotropic Blur\" for Color inputs, or \"Anisotropic Blur Grayscale\" for Grayscale inputs. ¶\n  P: Intensity {0.0 - 16.0}: Strength (Radius) of the blur. The higher this value, the further the blur will reach.\n  P: Anisotropy {0.0 - 1.0}: Directionality of the blur. Setting this to 0.0 is the same as performing a regular blur.\n  P: Angle {0.0 - 1.0}: Sets the angle for the blur direction.\n  P: Quality {0 - 1}: Switches between a box blur and an HQ blur internally. Trades in speed for quality.\n=== Blur HQ  [filters/blurs/blur-hq]  (Filters > Blurs)\nD: Performs a High-Quality gaussian blur on the result. Much better quality than the standard atomic box blur. Important: make sure to use the appropriate version for your input! Use \"Blur HQ\" for Color inputs, or \"Blur HQ Grayscale\" for Grayscale inputs. ¶\n  P: Intensity {0.0 - 16.0}: Strength (Radius) of the blur. The higher this value, the further the blur will reach.\n  P: Quality {0 - 1}: Increases internal sampling amount for even higher quality, at reduced computation speed.\n=== Blurs  [filters/blurs]  ()\n=== Median filter color  [filters/blurs/median-filter-color]  (Filters > Blurs)\nD: This filter smoothes out noise in an image while preserving edges. For every pixel, the node computes a color value according to the median value of the pixel's neighbours. ¶\nIN: Input (Color)\nOUT: Output\n  P: Kernel size {Integer}: A kernel is a specific group of values used in a filter’s computations. In this context, it is the values of the neighbouring pixels. For every pixel, the filter takes all neighbors around that pixel in a square kerne...\n  P: Filter type {Integer}: The computation applied to the neighbors sampled in the kernel. *- Median:* Use the median value of all neighbors directly. *- MLMAD:* Stands for 'Median Of Least Median Absolute Deviation'. The deviation accounts for...\n  P: Affect alpha {Boolean}: Controls whether the filter should be applied to the image's alpha channel. When *True*, the alpha channel is unchanged.\n=== Median filter grayscale  [filters/blurs/median-filter-grayscale]  (Filters > Blurs)\nD: This filter smoothes out noise in an image while preserving edges. For every pixel, the node computes a grayscale value according to the median value of the pixel's neighbours. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale)\nOUT: Output\n  P: Kernel size {Integer}: A kernel is a specific group of values used in a filter’s computations. In this context, it is the values of the neighbouring pixels. For every pixel, the filter takes all neighbors around that pixel in a square kerne...\n  P: Filter type {Integer}: The computation applied to the neighbors sampled in the kernel. *- Median:* Use the median value of all neighbors directly. *- MLMAD:* Stands for 'Median Of Least Median Absolute Deviation'. The deviation accounts for...\n=== MLV color  [filters/blurs/mlv-color]  (Filters > Blurs)\nD: MLV stands for &#39;Mean of Least Variance&#39;. This filter enhances edges and smoothes out noise in an image. The filter finds structuring areas in an image and uses them to both sharpen and flatten it. In some cases, this may result in steps along gradients wider than the structuring areas. ¶\nIN: Input (Color)\nOUT: Output\n  P: Intensity {Float}: The strength of the filtering applied to the image. Higher values results in more smoothing of details and noise into flatter areas.\n  P: Smoothness {Float}: The intensity of the sm