ty | Strength (radius) of the blur (0 to 16).\nk: Quality | Increases the internal sampling count for higher quality at lower speed.\nuse: Smoothing heights before normal or AO computation, building low-frequency versions for highpass, softening mask edges.\ncop: @blur with {Filter}=Gaussian (default) and {Size} for the radius; {Units}=Image keeps the result resolution independent like Designer. The relation between Intensity and {Size} is not documented (UNVERIFIED), so match visually.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## non-uniform-blur\nname: Non Uniform Blur\nsrc: /blurs/non-uniform-blur/\ncat: Texture filters > Blurs\nrel: high\npurpose: High-quality blur whose intensity is driven per pixel by an input mask, with optional anisotropy and asymmetry.\nk: Intensity | Maximum blur strength (0 to 50), masked by the Blur Map.\nk: Anisotropy | Adds directionality to the blur.\nk: Asymmetry | Adds a sampling bias along the Angle.\nk: Angle | Direction for anisotropy and bias.\nk: Samples | Number of samples, multiplied by Blades.\nk: Blades | Number of sampling sectors.\nuse: Blurring only certain areas of a height (for instance softening valleys but keeping crests sharp), variable-width softening of edges.\ncop: @blur has a per-pixel {i:size} input: wire the blur map there (scaled by the {Size} parameter or {Scale Size}). Directional variants use @streakblur with its {i:scalelength} input. UNVERIFIED for the anisotropy and asymmetry controls.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## anisotropic-blur\nname: Anisotropic Blur\nsrc: /blurs/anisotropic-blur/\ncat: Texture filters > Blurs\nrel: high\npurpose: High-quality directional (motion-like) blur with anisotropy and angle controls; anisotropy 0 equals a regular blur.\nk: Intensity | Strength (radius) of the blur (0 to 16).\nk: Anisotropy | Directionality of the blur (0 gives an ordinary blur).\nk: Angle | Direction of the blur.\nk: Quality | Switches between a box and an HQ blur internally.\nuse: Brushed metal streaks, wind-swept sand, long soft shadows in height maps, directional smoothing.\ncop: @streakblur ({Direction Type}=Angle, {Angle}, {Length}, {Mode}=Blur) is the directional blur (SideFX lists Directional Blur under Streak Blur); mix with a plain @blur through @blend to emulate anisotropy below 1. Relation of Anisotropy to the blend weight is UNVERIFIED.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## radial-blur\nname: Radial Blur\nsrc: /blurs/radial-blur/\ncat: Texture filters > Blurs\nrel: low\npurpose: Spinning, motion-type blur around a centre point.\nk: Samples | Quality of the blur (1 to 128).\nk: Angle | Amount of spin.\nk: Center Position | Centre point of the effect.\nuse: Brushed circular metal, lens-like spin streaks, turbine and wheel heights.\ncop: Build a tangent direction field and streak along it: @ramp with {Type}=Concentric at the centre, @slopedir with {Angle}=90 (directions that circle the hill instead of climbing it), then @distort with {Streak}=Blur and {Scale} for the amount. UNVERIFIED chain.\ngray: Grayscale input and output.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## median-filter-color\nname: Median Filter Color\nsrc: /blurs/median-filter-color/\ncat: Texture filters > Blurs\nrel: low\npurpose: Removes noise while preserving edges by replacing each pixel with the median of its neighbourhood (with a more robust MLMAD variant).\nk: Kernel size | Size of the square neighbourhood.\nk: Filter type | Median or MLMAD (median of least median absolute deviation).\nk: Affect alpha | Whether alpha is filtered too.\nuse: Cleaning scanned colour data before extracting heights.\ncop: @median ({Filter Size} 3x3 or 5x5, repeat for a larger kernel).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## median-filter-grayscale\nname: Median Filter Grayscale\nsrc: /blurs/median-filter-grayscale/\ncat: Texture filters > Blurs\nrel: medium\npurpose: Edge-preserving noise removal for grayscale data using the neighbourhood median.\nk: Kernel size | Size