{"parentUuid":"9b045b8f-21d7-444f-9f42-db9e40f687bc","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GSs1DuwEko24WD3UUYyucJ","type":"tool_result","content":"=== Flood Fill to Random Color | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/flood-fill-random-color/flood-fill-to-random-color.md\nGenerates tiles with random RGB colors from a Flood Fill base. Useful for adding color variation to tiles. ¶\n  IN: [] OUT: []\n=== Flood Fill to Random Grayscale | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/flood-fill-random-gra/flood-fill-to-random-grayscale.md\nGenerates random grayscale Luminance values from a Flood Fill base. Useful for adding Luminance variation to tiles. ¶\n  IN: [] OUT: []\n=== Flood Fill to GrayscaleColor | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/flood-fill-grayscale-col/flood-fill-to-grayscale-color.md\nUses Flood Fill data to generate grayscale or color value swatches. Unlike Flood Fill to Random Grayscale, these two nodes allow more control to set the exact variation and tones, with an additional extra input map to determine the base value to randomize on a per-cell basis. It's a powerfull system to give every cell a unique value or color, yet still retain control and base it off of a pre-determined input. ¶\n  IN: ['Flood Fill', 'Grayscale/Color Input'] OUT: []\n=== Directional distance | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/directional-distance/directional-distance.md\nDraws a distance gradient from the borders of a mask in a specified direction. Overlapping gradients are sorted by inverted normalized distance so that the distance to the closest border is drawn. The distance of the gradient can be dynamically adjusted along the border using a distance map. ¶\n  IN: ['Input', 'Distance map', 'Angle map'] OUT: ['Output', 'UV']\n=== Bevel smooth | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/bevel-smooth/bevel-smooth.md\nDraws a gradient or a flat color from the borders of a mask outward, inward or both. Overlapping gradients are sorted by inverted normalized distance so that the distance to the closest border is drawn. The distance of the gradient can be dynamically adjusted along the border using a distance map. ¶\n  IN: ['Mask input', 'Source input', 'Distance map'] OUT: ['Output', 'UV']\n=== Anisotropic Kuwahara Grayscale | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/anisotropic-kuwahara-gra/anisotropic-kuwahara-grayscale.md\nApplies an anisotropic directional blur which conforms to the details of the image. The result is an image which appears to *flow* in the direction of the shapes within. This adjustable blur computes or receives a *direction map* to determine that flow, which can be sharpened into flatter, more clearly defined areas. See also: Anisotropic Kuwahara color ¶\n  IN: ['Input', 'Anisotropy angle map', 'Slope map', 'Radius map (optional)', 'Direction map'] OUT: ['Output', 'Direction map']\n=== Reaction Diffusion Fast | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/reaction-diffusion-fast/reaction-diffusion-fast.md\nThis node performs a reaction-diffusion effect on an input grayscale image. Reaction-diffusion is a process in which matter spreads out (diffuses) and interacts (reacts) with other matter. It is a mathematical model that simulates what happens in nature when certain patterns are formed on animal skin for example. This node is optimised for performance and does make some accuracy trade-offs for speed. ¶\n  IN: ['Input'] OUT: ['Output']\n=== Mosaic | Filters > Effects | filters/effects/mosaic/mosaic.md\n\"Facetises\" an existing, smooth, sloping gradient map by performing a multi-pass Warp effect. When the same map is used for both inputs, it essentially grows and accentuates the brightest areas. This is useful for adding more definition to grayscale maps such as Heightmap, as it can introduce more definition to shapes. ¶\n  IN: ['Color', 'Mosaic Map'] OUT: []","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"7d5a576e-de2d-422f-8777