e between Fore- and Background inputs. Subtracts Background from Foreground, returning an absolute result (never a negative value). ¶\nIN: Background (Color Input); Foreground (Color Input); Mask (Grayscale Input)\n  P: Opacity {0.0 - 1.0}: Blending Opacity between Foreground and Background.\n  P: Alpha Blending {False/True}: Toggles blending of the Foreground and Background alpha channels. If set to False, the alpha channel of the foreground is ignored.\n=== Dissolve  [filters/blending/dissolve]  (Filters > Blending)\nD: Blends two inputs together with White Noise as mask for the transition. ¶\nIN: Foreground (Color Input); Background (Color Input); Mask (Grayscale Input)\n  P: Opacity {0.0 - 1.0}: Blending Opacity between Foreground and Background.\n  P: Alpha Blending {False/True}: Toggles blending of the Foreground and Background alpha channels. If set to False, the alpha channel of the foreground is ignored.\n=== Linear Burn  [filters/blending/linear-burn]  (Filters > Blending)\nD: Performs a Linear Burn blend. Mathematical formula is Foreground + Background - 1. ¶\nIN: Foreground (Color Input); Background (Color Input); Mask (Grayscale Input)\n  P: Opacity {0.0 - 1.0}: Blending Opacity between Foreground and Background.\n  P: Alpha Blending {False/True}: Toggles blending of the Foreground and Background alpha channels. If set to False, the alpha channel of the foreground is ignored.\n=== Luminosity (Blend Node)  [filters/blending/luminosity-blend-node]  (Filters > Blending)\nD: Performs a Luminosity blend mode, which preserves the hue and chrominance of the Background, while adopting the luminance of the Foreground. ¶\nIN: Foreground (Color Input); Background (Color Input); Mask (Grayscale Input)\n  P: Opacity {0.0 - 1.0}: Blending Opacity between Foreground and Background.\n  P: Alpha Blending {False/True}: Toggles blending of the Foreground and Background alpha channels. If set to False, the alpha channel of the foreground is ignored.\n=== Multi Switch  [filters/blending/multi-switch]  (Filters > Blending)\nD: Acts as a switch-box, only passing through the input defined by the 'Input Selection' parameter. So if two Inputs are connected, only one of those will be returned (unmodified), depending on the user's choice. Very useful for adding many different options in a graph. Combined with exposing (preferably as a Drop Down List), a lot of customisation is possible. Important: make sure to use the appropriate version for \nIN: Input 1-20 (Color Input)\n  P: Input Number {2 - 20}: Amount of inputs to expose. Important: does not remove connections when the number is reduced!\n  P: Input Selection {1 - 20}: Which input to return as the result.\n=== Switch  [filters/blending/switch]  (Filters > Blending)\nD: A simple 2-position switch node. Returns either Input 1 or Input 2 based on the Switch parameter setting. Result is unmodified. See Multi Switch for a more advanced version. Very useful for exposing a boolean (True/False) choice in a graph, where you only need a single button and not a complex drop-down list for a whole selection of options. Important: make sure to use the appropriate version for your input! Use \"\nIN: Input 1 (True) (Color or Grayscale Input); Input 2 (False) (Color or Grayscale Input)\n  P: Switch {False/True}: Switches between Input 1 (True) and 2 (False).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"dd0c5b0e-b3d7-4b33-8cde-a9559466d508","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:43:25.225Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ff66e472-1c15-43ea-b8b3-3c7315292470","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"22256492-446e-4242-a4ae-eed9a9be2be0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessio