{"parentUuid":"23e435ea-0610-4169-ab7b-7788979274d7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvdM8CfBToVoDuMPMpL1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01P3qPhFpFC9QFKDy78FGMJZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/06_normal_tiling_channels.txt","content":"## normal-sobel\nname: Normal Sobel\nsrc: normal-sobel.md\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Converts a height map to a normal map with Sobel sampling, a slightly more advanced version of the atomic Normal node.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the converted normals (0 to 3).\nk: Normal Format | OpenGL or DirectX green channel convention.\nuse: Normal map from a procedural height for export and for lighting checks; Sobel sampling gives smoother results on noisy heights.\ncop: @heighttonormal: {Height Scale} is the intensity, {Normal Type}=Offset (0 to 1) for exportable normal maps, {Kernel Size} larger than 1 smooths like a wider derivative; the green flip for DirectX is done by @channelswap or a negative component with @wrangle. UNVERIFIED about the Sobel kernel match.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## height-to-normal-world-units\nname: Height to Normal World Units\nsrc: height-normal-world-units/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Height to normal conversion that uses real-world dimensions (surface size and maximum depth in centimetres) for the most accurate normals, as for scanned material.\nk: Surface Size (cm) | Physical dimensions of the input height map.\nk: Height Depth (cm) | Maximum depth of the height details.\nk: Normal Format | OpenGL or DirectX convention.\nk: Sampling | Standard or Sobel sampling mode.\nuse: Physically scaled normals from a height whose real size is known.\ncop: @heighttonormal with {Height Scale} derived from the ratio Height Depth over Surface Size (the exact definition of the COP scale is relative to the image coordinate system, so verify visually; UNVERIFIED); the @slopedir {Space} option (Image, Pixel, Local) shows that COP can also compute slopes in local (world pixel) units.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## height-normal-blender\nname: Height Normal Blender\nsrc: /normal-map/height-normal-blender/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Shortcut that converts a height map to normals internally and blends it correctly onto a normal map.\nk: Normal Intensity | Intensity of the height to normal conversion (0 to 16).\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL convention.\nuse: Adding procedural micro detail (scratches, grain) from a height onto a base normal map.\ncop: @heighttonormal ({Height Scale}) feeding the {i:fg} input of @combinenormals with the base normal on {i:bg}; {Combine Method}=Reoriented or Whiteout for correct detail blending.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-blend\nname: Normal Blend\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-blend/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: medium\npurpose: Blends two normal maps with an optional mask while keeping the vectors normalised; not intended for overlaying details.\nk: Opacity | Blend opacity between foreground and background.\nk: Use Mask | Enables the Mask input.\nuse: Switching between two normal maps by a mask (for example two materials).\ncop: @blend ({Mode}=Blend) with the {i:mask} input, then renormalise (@convertnormal has a {Normalize} option).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-combine\nname: Normal Combine\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-combine/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Combines the details of two normal maps in a mathematically correct way, like an overlay for normals, with three techniques trading speed for quality.\nk: Technique | Whiteout (low quality), Channel mixer (high quality) or Detail oriented (high quality).\nuse: Stacking a detail normal over a base normal (tile micro detail over a large relief).\ncop: @combinenormals with {Combine Method}: Whiteout (matches the SD whiteout), UDN, Reoriented (the detail-preserving method; probably closest to SD's detail oriented, UNVERIFIED); both inputs must share type and space, so convert with @convertnormal first if needed.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-invert\nname: Normal Invert\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-invert/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Inverts any combination of the red, green, blue and alpha channels of a normal map, typically to switch between DirectX and OpenGL.\nk: Invert Red | Inverts the red channel.\nk: Invert Green | Inverts the green channel (DirectX versus OpenGL).\nk: Invert Blue | Inverts the blue channel.\nk: Invert Alpha | Inverts the alpha channel.\nuse: Fixing the Y direction of exported normals.\ncop: @invert with only the green channel active (use {Toggle RGB}, {Red}, {Green}, {Blue}, {Alpha} toggles of the Mask group), or @channelswap.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-normalize\nname: Normal Normalize\nsrc: normal-normalize.md\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Re-normalises every normal vector of the image to unit length.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Cleaning a normal map after many blends before export.\ncop: @convertnormal with {Normalize} on (the node converts between signed and offset and can normalise).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-to-height\nname: Normal to Height\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-to-height/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Attempts to rebuild a height map from a tangent-space normal map (simpler version; information is inevitably lost).\nk: Relief Balance | How much each frequency influences the result.\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nk: Global Opacity | Global opacity of the effect.\nuse: Recovering a height from a scanned or painted normal map to re-use with other height operations.\ncop: @normaltoheight with {Solver}=FFT (fast, periodic) or Poisson, {Normal Type} Offset or Signed, {Height Scale}, {Center}, and {X Borders}/{Y Borders} (Open, Closed, Periodic).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-to-height-hq\nname: Normal To Height HQ\nsrc: normal-to-height-hq.md\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: high\npurpose: Higher-quality normal to height reconstruction with frequency bias, intensity, normalisation and quality controls.\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nk: Relief Balance | Blend between low and high frequency bias.\nk: Height Intensity | Multiplier of the resulting height.\nk: Height Normalize | Auto-levels the result to full contrast.\nk: Quality | Speed versus quality.\nuse: Best-effort height recovery from normal-only sources.\ncop: @normaltoheight with {Solver}=Poisson and many {Iterations} for accuracy; {Smoothing Method} Gauss-Seidel or Jacobi; finish with @equalize for the normalisation.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-transform\nname: Normal Transform\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-transform/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Transforms a normal map in 2D while recalculating tangent space so the result stays a valid normal map.\nk: Matrix2x2 | Rotation or scale of the input.\nk: Offset | Translation of the result.\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nuse: Rotating or scaling a detail normal map without breaking its lighting.\ncop: @xform2d for the pixel transform followed by @vectorxform (rotate the vectors by the same angle) so the normals stay consistent.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-vector-rotation\nname: Normal Vector Rotation\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-vector-rotation/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Rotates the vectors of a normal map in tangent space without moving pixels, optionally with random rotation per grayscale facet.\nk: Rotation Angle | Angle by which the vectors are rotated.\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nuse: Adjusting the light direction response of a normal map or randomising facets.\ncop: @vectorxform ({Rotate}) on the signed normal vectors; the per-facet map would drive a per-pixel angle through @wrangle.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## normal-uncombine\nname: Normal Uncombine\nsrc: /normal-map/normal-uncombine/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Removes from a normal map the surface detail described by a height map.\nk: Normal format | Format of the input normal map.\nuse: Separating a base normal from a baked-in detail.\ncop: @heighttonormal on the height, then the inverse of the combine step (in tangent space subtract the xy components when signed, or use @combinenormals on a negated detail). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## facing-normal\nname: Facing Normal\nsrc: facing-normal.md\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Outputs a grayscale image showing how much the normals face the viewer in texture space.\nnote: The documentation page lists no parameters.\nuse: Slope masks (flat areas are bright, steep areas dark).\ncop: The blue (Z) component of the signed normal: @convertnormal to Signed then @channelextract of the blue channel; or @dot with a constant (0,0,1).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## bent-normal\nname: Bent Normal\nsrc: /normal-map/bent-normal/\ncat: Texture filters > Normal map\nrel: low\npurpose: Generates a bent normal map from a height map: a normal map with ambient occlusion information embedded in the direction of the average unoccluded ray.\nk: Use Physical Size | Uses physical size to determine the height scale.\nk: Samples | Number of rays.\nk: Height Scale | Multiplier of the input height.\nk: Distribution | Falloff distribution.\nk: Maximum Distance | Maximum ray travel distance.\nk: Spread Angle | Spread angle of the rays.\nk: Normal Format | Inverts the green channel of the output.\nuse: Real-time engines that bake AO direction into the normal.\ncop: No direct node. @heighttonormal for the base normal and a custom hemisphere average with @opencl or @wrangle; @heighttoambientocclusion provides only the scalar AO. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\