{"parentUuid":"43a99e46-b79f-4d36-93b1-c563fd8e95d6","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHwNrHVG3eA5J1Dr9FN9u5","type":"tool_result","content":"#nodes 8\n=== Blend  [atomic-nodes/blend]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Combines two images using a specified blending mode and an optional mask. It is the most useful node of all Atomic nodes, almost any Graph you build in Substance 3D Designer will make use of this node. ¶\nIN: Foreground (Grayscale/Color); Background (Grayscale/Color [primary]); Opacity (Grayscale)\n  P: Opacity {Float}: Opacity of the Foreground layer blending into the Background. It works independent to the Opacity input and acts as an additional multiplier to it.\n  P: Blending mode {Integer [static]}: Sets the blending operation to be used. See the dedicated page about blending modes.\n  P: Alpha blending {Integer [static]}: Determines blending behavior when color inputs have Alpha channels: Use source alpha Ignore alpha Straight alpha blending Premultiplied alpha blending\n  P: Cropping area {Float4 [static]}: Allow for setting a custom cropping region that behaves like an additional Opacity mask. Any area cropped shows only the background.\n=== Blur  [atomic-nodes/blur]  ()\nD: The Blur Node performs a \"box-blur\" operation: averaging the values of pixels over a set distance, resulting in a fuzzy, unsharp look. It provides the simplest, fastest and most basic blurring operation available in Substance 3D Designer. While blur works well for fast, simple operations, such as slightly softening some edges, in any more demanding scenario Blur HQ is a better choice, trading off performance for q\n  P: Blur (Atomic) {}: Blur HQ\n=== Channels shuffle  [atomic-nodes/channel-shuffle]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Rearranges the color channels of one or two input images into the output image. I.e., takes two inputs and allows you to return an output where any of the Red, Green, Blue and Alpha channels are swapped or set to any of the channels from the input. Essentially it allows you to pack and swap RGB channels in any possible way. Grayscale inputs are treated as if they are Color: Red, Green, Blue and Alpha all return\nIN: Input 1 (Color/Grayscale [primary]); Input 2 (Color/Grayscale)\n  P: Red channel {Integer}: Choose the source channel to be inserted into the output image's Red channel.\n  P: Green channel {Integer}: Choose the source channel to be inserted into the output image's Green channel.\n  P: Blue channel {Integer}: Choose the source channel to be inserted into the output image's Blue channel.\n  P: Alpha channel {Integer}: Choose the source channel to be inserted into the output image's Alpha channel.\n=== Curve  [atomic-nodes/curve]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Remaps the values in an image using a custom curve. The node provides an interface to image tonality remapping, similar to other 2D image editing applications. The user can place points and adjust Bezier curves to remap the input, which can be either grayscale or color.It is especially useful when used with gradient transitions to remap them to a specific height profile, it allows for very precise modeling of b\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary])\n  P: Apply/Expose curve {Boolean}: Allows to copy the user curve to the output instead of applying it to the input image\n  P: Curve addressing {Boolean}: This parameter determines how HDR pixels out of the &#91;0, 1&#93; range in the input are handled: clamped or fold up to &#91;0, 1&#93;.\n  P: Curve {Array of curve keys}: The custom curve used to map the input grayscale values. May be edited using the Curve editor.\n  NOTE Create and move a point: In order to create a point, simply double-click anywhere on the Curve view:\n  NOTE Controlling the point influence: In order to obtain precise results, the curve nodes offers different modes for each point:\n  NOTE Show input histogram: You can show/hide the histogram of your input just by clicking on !\n  NOTE Controlling each channel individually (color input): When you input is a color node, you have the ability to ajust the curve for each channel: Just select the curve you want to ajust in the dropdown list located on the top right: While in RGB curve mode, you canhide/show the individual channel curves by pressing/unpressing !:\n  NOTE Aligning, mirroring and flipping: If you right click on the curve view, you will get some more options.\n  NOTE Keyboard Shortcuts: Draw a selection box. Constrain the movement on the X or Y axis. Temporarily break the handles to move them independently.\n  NOTE Adjust the framing of the curve: While tweaking the handlers, you may be in a case where one handler is going over the curve view. In that case, you can use the ! button to fit the size to the content. The ! button resets the zoom level to 1\n=== Directional blur  [atomic-nodes/directional-blur]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Applies blurring in a specified direction according to an intensity map. This node performs an operation similar to a motion blur on an input. Unlike the regular 'Blur' node, which blurs equally in all directions, 'Directional blur' works along a user-defined angle. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary])\n  P: Intensity {Float}: Sets the blurring radius in pixels.\n  P: Angle {Float}: The direction of the blur effect in number of turns clockwise, starting from horizontal – I.e., direction vector (1, 0).\n  NOTE Directional and Anisotropic blur: This images below show the Directional blur and the Anisotropic blur in effect on the same input shape, with similar parameters. Anisotropic Blur has been set to full anisotropy and high quality.\n=== Directional warp  [atomic-nodes/directional-warp]  (Atomic Nodes**)\nD: ¶ Displaces pixels in a specified direction according to an intensity map, which can result in deformation. Warps an input in a user-set direction, multiplied by a user-set Intensity map. It works similar to Warp, but then only in a specific direction. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary]); Intensity input (Grayscale)\n  P: Intensity {Float}: Sets the intensity of the warp.\n  P: Warp angle {Float}: Sets the angle of the warp effect, in number of turns..\n  P: Input filtering mode {Boolean}: Controls whether nearest or bilinear filtering is used to sample the Input.\n  P: Intensity map offset {Float}: This value is subtracted from the the Intensity input image values.\n=== Distance  [atomic-nodes/distance]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Finds the position of the nearest white pixel in a mask and outputs a gradient from that position, or the color at that position in a source image. This node creates an outward linear fade (gradient) from any pixels in the input max over 0.5 grayscale value. ¶\nIN: Mask input (Grayscale [primary]); Source input (Color/Grayscale)\n  P: Color mode {Boolean}: Toggles between a grayscale and a color output image. Also changes the 'Source input' input type.\n  P: Maximum distance {Float}: Adjusts the maximum distance for detecting the closest border in the mask, in pixels.\n  P: Combine source/distance {Boolean}: Determine how the optional 'Source input'is combined with the final cells. Combine: Combines the 'Source input' value with the fading linear mask. If the 'Source input' input is connected, its value is combined with computed di...\n  P: Distance mode {Integer}: Selects the method computing the distance to the closest border in the extracted mask: Euclidean: Sum of squared X/Y differences. Manhattan: Sum of absolute values of X/Y differences. Chebyshev: Maximum of absolute values of X/...\n=== Emboss  [atomic-nodes/emboss]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Applies an embossing effect by lighting the sides of shapes in an image according to a specified light source direction. I.e., the node performs a simple 2D shading based on 2 inputs, simulating light falling on a surface with height and depth variation. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary]); Intensity input (Grayscale)\n  P: Intensity {Float}: Adjusts the global intensity of illumination effect. Sets the intensity of the of the \"height\" map and thus the strength of the lighting effect\n  P: Light angle {Float}: Sets the angle at which the light is simulated. Defines the illumination angle of the embossed image's highlight\n  P: Highlight color {Float/Float4}: Sets the color of areas facing towards the light angle. Sets the color of the highlight if the input image is color.\n  P: Shadow color {Float/Float4}: Sets the color of areas facing away from the light angle. Sets the color of the shadowed regions of the embossed image.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"aed21888-0309-4bcb-ab13-6892ed3dfe84","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:45:27.521Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"57ac16fd-df8f-4710-a003-bff44dd876fd","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"43a99e46-b79f-4d36-93b1-c563fd8e95d6","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
