 your graphs. Users can also select settings like Font, Alignment and rotation to customize the text placement. The Text node is very powerful and the only way to easily place text. It can be a bit tricky to use due to placement always happening on a limited, square canvas, and fonts being driven by a system-define\nIN: Background (Grayscale/Color [primary])\n  P: Color mode {Boolean}: Toggles between a grayscale and a color output image.\n  P: Text {String}: Determines the text description.\n  P: Font {String}: The font resource used to render the text.\n  P: Font size {Float}: The font size for the text in points.\n  P: Alignment {Integer}: Sets the text alignment as left, center (default) or right.\n  P: Transformation {Float4}: The 2x2 transformation matrix applied to the rendered text.\n  P: Position {Float2}: The position of the text in the output image.\n  P: Background {Float/Float4}: The output image's background color.\n  P: Font color {Float/Float4}: The color of the text.\n=== Transformation 2D  [atomic-nodes/transformation-2d]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Applies a 2D transformation matrix to an image: translation, rotation, scaling, symmetry and shearing. It is quite similar to Transforming (Ctrl-T) in Photoshop, or using the 2D mapping manipulator in Substance 3D Painter. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary])\n  P: Transformation matrix {Float4}: Open the underlying transform matrix for direct editing. Allows you to change rotation and scaling. Can also be adjusted through the gizmo in the 2D view. Warning: they do not directly co...\n  P: Offset {Float2}: Defines the 2D displacement of the image. Allows you to change the position or offset Can also be adjusted through the gizmo in the 2D view. Does relate directly to the 2D view output.\n  P: Mipmap mode {Integer}: Allows you to switch to a manual mipmap level, which reduces artifacts in an image using texture filtering.\n  P: Mipmap level {Integer}: Sets the mipmap level to be used. *Available when 'Mipmap mode' is set to 'Manual'*\n  P: Matte color {Float4}: The color used as background when tiling of transformation is disabled. I.e., sets the color used when the transformed input does not cover an area of the output. Can be made transparent ...\n  P: Filtering {Integer}: Sets the downsampling method used. Does not work particularly well with Mipmap Level being reduced.\n=== Uniform color  [atomic-nodes/uniform-color]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Generates a flat grayscale or color value. It is a simple node that is used very often as a starting point for adding colors or creating specific values. ¶\n  P: Color mode {Boolean}: Toggles between a grayscale and a color output image.\n  P: Output color {Float/Float4}: Selects the flat color to use in the output image. When using the 'Color' color mode, the Alpha channel is used for opacity where 0 is fully transparent and 1 is fully opaque..\n=== Warp  [atomic-nodes/warp]  (Atomic Nodes)\nD: ¶ Displaces the pixel values in the input image according to the slopes computed from a separate gradient input, resulting in deformation. Unlike the Directional Warp this node pushes away uniformly from white areas, in a direction defined by the slope or gradient of the Gradient Input. ¶\nIN: Input (Grayscale/Color [primary]); Gradient input (Grayscale)\n  P: Intensity {Float}: Sets the intensity of the warp.\n  P: Input filtering mode {Boolean}: Controls whether nearest or bilinear filtering is used to sample the Input.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d8d8568c-e55a-42b9-b4b8-d14421db1cda","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:45:33.382Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c9364280-fc6b-4332-8410-d8ba9025afe1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a8b33a66-8fa7-412f-9400-8a8e69884396","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"