{"parentUuid":"cebc6305-8ac8-4be5-b1c0-9e5626c87074","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfve1kTJ8irBQrXHwiBG5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3834,"cache_read_input_tokens":699898,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3834,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":11848,"serverClassifierRequest":"968334d4-ddff-412b-9e62-bef70237e33f","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cfve1h6tuy71aPFFvPG4z","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"7c0b8015-b66a-404a-bce9-8f87e16ea888","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:52:30.552Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7c0b8015-b66a-404a-bce9-8f87e16ea888","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfve1kTJ8irBQrXHwiBG5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016mRWbhaczz8C5vqZMUHE2R","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/08_atomic.txt","content":"## atomic-blend\nname: Blend\nsrc: atomic-nodes/blend/blend.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Combines a foreground and a background with a blending mode and an optional opacity mask; the most useful atomic node, comparable to two stacked layers with a blend mode in Photoshop or Painter.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground, multiplied with the Opacity input.\nk: Blending mode | Blend operation: Copy, Add (linear dodge), Subtract, Multiply, Add Sub, Max (lighten), Min (darken), Switch, Divide, Overlay, Screen, Soft light.\nk: Alpha blending | How alpha is treated for colour inputs (use source alpha, ignore alpha, straight or premultiplied blending).\nk: Cropping area | Crop region that acts like an extra opacity mask.\nuse: Everything in a height graph: Add or Max to stack bumps, Subtract to carve, Min to cut, Multiply to mask, Copy with a mask to composite two heights; both inputs must be of the same type (grayscale or colour).\ncop: @blend. SD mode to COP {Mode}: Copy is Over (or Blend with the mask), Add is Add, Subtract is Subtract (check operand order, or use {Swap FG and BG}), Multiply is Multiply, Max is Maximum, Min is Minimum, Divide is Divide, Overlay is Overlay, Screen is Screen, Soft light is SoftLight; Add Sub (foreground above 0.5 adds, below subtracts) has no mode: remap the foreground from 0..1 to -1..1 with @remap and use Add (UNVERIFIED). Opacity is the {Mask} amount and the {i:mask} input; Switch is @switch.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## blend-modes\nname: Blending modes (Blend node reference)\nsrc: blending-modes-description.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Reference page for the twelve modes of the Blend node: Copy, Add (linear dodge), Subtract, Multiply, Add Sub, Max (lighten), Min (darken), Switch, Divide, Overlay, Screen and Soft light.\nnote: Not a node; documented because every height graph relies on these modes. The Switch mode skips computing the unused branch, which can speed up a graph.\nuse: Choosing the right mode: Add and Subtract for additive and carving heights, Max and Min for union and intersection of shapes, Multiply for masks, Overlay and Soft light for detail contrast, Divide for flattening.\ncop: @blend {Mode} lists Blend, Over, Under, Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Screen, Hypot, Difference, Exclusion, Sharpen, Maximum, Minimum, Overlay, SoftLight, HardLight, Dodge, Burn, Hue, Saturation, Luminosity, Color; the extra COP modes Hypot, Exclusion and HardLight have no SD Blend counterpart. Use @average (Layer Merge, {Operation} Add, Average, Multiply, Min, Max, Over) to merge many layers at once.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-blur\nname: Blur\nsrc: atomic-nodes/blur/blur.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Fast, basic box blur; for anything demanding the documentation recommends Blur HQ.\nkx: Intensity | 0 to unlimited | Distance of the blur; at high values the image turns into its average colour.\nuse: Quick softening of masks or heights where quality is not critical.\ncop: @blur with {Filter}=Box.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-directional-blur\nname: Directional Blur\nsrc: atomic-nodes/directional-blur/directional-blur.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Motion-blur like blur along a chosen direction; unlike Blur it works along a user-defined angle.\nk: Intensity | Blur radius in pixels.\nk: Angle | Direction in turns, clockwise from horizontal.\nuse: Brushed metal, wind streaks, softening edges along one axis, building anisotropic grain from noise.\ncop: @streakblur (SideFX-confirmed): {Direction Type}=Angle, {Angle} in degrees (SD uses turns, multiply by 360), {Length} for Intensity, {Units}=Pixels, {Mode}=Blur.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-directional-warp\nname: Directional Warp\nsrc: atomic-nodes/directional-warp/directional-warp.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Displaces pixels in one chosen direction, scaled per pixel by an intensity map, which deforms the input.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the warp.\nk: Warp angle | Direction of the warp, in turns.\nk: Input filtering mode | Nearest or bilinear sampling of the input.\nk: Intensity map offset | Value subtracted from the intensity input.\nuse: Pushing shapes sideways by a mask, wobbling edges, streaking, the engine inside Slope Blur and Multi Directional Warp.\ncop: @distort (SideFX lists the Warp node under Distort): {Direction Type}=Angle with {Angle}, {Scale} for Intensity, the intensity map on the {i:scale} input (which multiplies {Scale}); subtract the map offset beforehand with @bright {Shift}. {Filter} chooses the sampling.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-warp\nname: Warp\nsrc: atomic-nodes/warp/warp.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Displaces pixels according to the slopes of a separate gradient input, pushing uniformly away from white areas.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the warp.\nk: Input filtering mode | Nearest or bilinear sampling of the input.\nuse: Distorting patterns by a height map (organic edges, cracked lines following a noise), the most common distortion of tiles and cracks.\ncop: @distort (SideFX-confirmed) with the Distort by Slope recipe: @slopedir on the gradient map feeds the {i:dir} input; the help describes the result as pushing away from larger values toward lower ones, which matches the SD behaviour. {Scale} is Intensity, {Streak}=Off.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-distance\nname: Distance\nsrc: atomic-nodes/distance/distance.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Finds the nearest white pixel of a mask and outputs a linear gradient fading from it (or the colour of the nearest pixel from a source image).\nk: Color mode | Grayscale or colour output.\nk: Maximum distance | Maximum distance searched, in pixels.\nk: Combine source/distance | How the optional source is combined with the distance fade.\nk: Distance mode | Euclidean, Manhattan or Chebyshev metric.\nuse: Bevels, rounded edges, joint gradients, rust and dirt halos from a binary mask; the common recipe is Distance then Histogram Scan.\ncop: SideFX-confirmed: @feather and @extrapolateboundaries (the Distance page of SideFX). Set {Feather Direction}=Circle (Euclidean), Diamond (Manhattan) or Square (Chebyshev), {Distance for Unit Change} equal to the Maximum distance, and enable {Feather from High to Low} to grow outward; for exact Euclidean distance an alternative is @monotosdf then @sdftomono with {Iso Range}. The colour-from-nearest-pixel mode is @extrapolateboundaries.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-emboss\nname: Emboss\nsrc: atomic-nodes/emboss/emboss.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Applies a 2D embossing effect by lighting the sides of shapes according to a light direction, using a height input.\nk: Intensity | Global intensity of the effect.\nk: Light angle | Angle of the simulated light.\nk: Highlight color | Colour of areas facing the light.\nk: Shadow color | Colour of areas facing away.\nuse: Quick visual read of a height, faked baked lighting.\ncop: @derivative (outputs {o:dCdx} and {o:dCdy}) combined with the light direction cos and sin in @wrangle (or @heighttonormal into @light with {Directional} on). Equivalence of the exact formula is UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-normal\nname: Normal\nsrc: atomic-nodes/normal/normal.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Computes a tangent-space normal map from a grayscale height map.\nk: Intensity | Height intensity used for the conversion.\nk: Normal format | OpenGL or DirectX convention (Y flip).\nk: Alpha channel content | Alpha filled with the input or forced to 1.\nuse: The standard normal output of every height graph.\ncop: @heighttonormal: {Height Scale} for Intensity, {Normal Type}=Offset (0 to 1) for an exportable normal map, {Kernel Size} to smooth stepping artefacts; the Y flip for DirectX is a green channel inversion (@channelswap or @invert on green only).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-levels\nname: Levels\nsrc: atomic-nodes/levels/levels.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Remaps tonal range and colour balance with input low, mid and high and output low and high controls, in a histogram interface.\nkx: Level in low | Float or Float4 | Input values that become full black.\nkx: Level in high | Float or Float4 | Input values that become full white.\nkx: Level in mid | Float or Float4 | Input value that becomes mid grey (a gamma-like control).\nkx: Level out low | Float or Float4 | Lower clamp of the output.\nkx: Level out high | Float or Float4 | Upper clamp of the output.\nkx: Intermediary clamp | Boolean | Clamps the transformed input to 0..1 before computing the output levels.\nuse: Fine control of ranges everywhere: normalising a noise, inverting by swapping output levels, setting heights to a physical range.\ncop: @remap (SideFX: Levels node is Remap): {Input Min} and {Input Max} are Level in low and high, {Output Min} and {Output Max} are Level out low and high, {Outside Range}=Clamp at End is the intermediary clamp; the midpoint is a separate @gamma with exponent ln(0.5) divided by ln(m), where m is the mid level normalised between low and high (UNVERIFIED formula).\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-curve\nname: Curve\nsrc: atomic-nodes/curve/curve.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Remaps values with a custom Bezier curve (grayscale or per colour channel), perfect for shaping gradients into specific height profiles.\nk: Apply/Expose curve | Outputs the curve itself instead of applying it.\nk: Curve addressing | How HDR values outside 0..1 are handled (clamped or folded).\nk: Curve | The curve keys.\nuse: Bevel and edge profiles, rounding a gradient, custom falloff of masks, converting a linear distance into a pipe or ogee profile.\ncop: @remap with {Operation}=Remap, {Use Ramp} on and a mono {Ramp} drawn as the curve (set bezier or spline point interpolation); per-channel colour curves use @channelsplit and one @remap per channel.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-gradient-map\nname: Gradient Map\nsrc: atomic-nodes/gradient-map/gradient-map.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Remaps grayscale values through a custom colour gradient, either to colourise or simply to convert grayscale to colour.\nkx: Color mode | Boolean | Colour or grayscale output.\nkx: Gradient adressing | Boolean | Repeat or clamp values outside 0..1.\nkx: Gradient | Array of gradient keys | The gradient ramp used for the mapping.\nuse: Colourising heights for preview or albedo; output a heat map of a height.\ncop: @monotorgb (passes a mono layer through a colour ramp: {Ramp}, {Input Min}, {Input Max}, {Outside Range} Clamp at End or Repeat for the addressing). SideFX lists Gradient nodes under Ramp nodes.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-gradient-dynamic\nname: Gradient (Dynamic)\nsrc: atomic-nodes/gradient-dynamic/gradient-dynamic.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Gradient Map whose colour keys come from a row or column of pixels of another image instead of being stored in the node.\nk: Gradient addressing | Repeat or clamp outside the 0..1 range.\nk: Gradient orientation | Sample a row (horizontal) or column (vertical) of the gradient image.\nk: Gradient input position | Normalised position of the sampled row or column.\nuse: Data-driven colour or profile ramps, colour palettes picked from textures.\ncop: @monotorgb (or @remap for a mono result) with the gradient wired into the {i:ramp} input; crop the wanted row with @crop and rotate columns into rows with @xform2d.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-grayscale-conversion\nname: Grayscale Conversion\nsrc: atomic-nodes/grayscale-conversion/grayscale-conversion.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Converts colour to grayscale by weighting the channels; setting a single weight to 1 extracts one channel quickly.\nk: Channel weights | Weight of each RGBA channel (even split by default).\nk: Flatten alpha | Multiplies the result by the alpha.\nk: Background value | Value treated as transparent when flattening.\nuse: Extracting a height or mask from a coloured scan or a packed texture channel.\ncop: @mono with {Operation}=Custom and {Weights} (and {Normalize Weights}); a single channel is @channelextract.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-hsl\nname: HSL\nsrc: atomic-nodes/hsl/hsl.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Adjusts hue, saturation and lightness of a colour image.\nk: Hue | Hue shift (0.5 is neutral).\nk: Saturation | Saturation (0.5 is neutral).\nk: Lightness | Lightness (0.5 is neutral).\nuse: Colour grading; not used on heights.\ncop: @hsv (SideFX: HSV nodes where value replaces lightness): {Operation}=Hue/Sat/Val Adjust with {Hue Shift}, {Saturation Scale}, {Value Scale}.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-sharpen\nname: Sharpen\nsrc: atomic-nodes/sharpen/sharpen.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Unsharp-mask style sharpening, good for base colour maps but to be avoided on normal and metallic maps.\nkx: Intensity | Float | Strength of the sharpening.\nkx: Punchthrough Alpha | Boolean | Whether alpha is sharpened too (colour input).\nuse: Final crispness on heights after blurs; careful because it can overshoot and create halos.\ncop: @sharpen ({Size}, {Amplitude}, {Gain}, {Threshold}).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-transformation-2d\nname: Transformation 2D\nsrc: atomic-nodes/transformation-2d/transformation-2d.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Applies a 2D transformation matrix (translation, rotation, scale, symmetry, shear) to an image, like free transform in Photoshop.\nk: Transformation matrix | The 2x2 matrix (rotation and scale).\nk: Offset | Displacement of the image.\nk: Mipmap mode | Automatic or manual mipmapping.\nk: Mipmap level | Manual mipmap level.\nk: Matte color | Fill colour when tiling is off.\nk: Filtering | Downsampling method.\nuse: Scaling, rotating and offsetting patterns; tiling must be preserved with the Safe Transform node instead.\ncop: @xform2d (SideFX-confirmed correspondence of 2D transforms): {Translate}, {Rotate}, {Scale} or {Uniform Scale}, {Shear}, {Pivot Translate}, {Border}=Wrap for tiling or Constant with the matte value, {Filter} for sampling.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-uniform-color\nname: Uniform Color\nsrc: atomic-nodes/uniform-color/uniform-color.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Generates a flat grayscale or colour value; constantly used as a starting point or a mask.\nk: Color mode | Grayscale or colour output.\nk: Output color | The flat value (alpha is opacity in colour mode).\nuse: Base level for heights, offsets (blend a constant to shift a height), flat masks.\ncop: @constant (SideFX-confirmed): {Signature}=Mono for grayscale, {Value} for the level.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## constant-value\nname: Constant\nsrc: values/constant.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Value node that holds a constant (float, integer, vector...) usable as a parameter source in a graph.\nnote: This page is under the Values section of the library and lists no parameters.\nuse: Driving several nodes from one number.\ncop: A parameter on a parent node with channel references (the Houdini way of sharing one value), or a promoted parameter of the HDA.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-bitmap\nname: Bitmap\nsrc: atomic-nodes/bitmap/bitmap.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Loads (links or imports) a bitmap resource into the graph, or creates a bitmap for the painting tools.\nk: Color mode | Colour or grayscale output.\nk: PKG resource path | Reference to the bitmap resource.\nk: Resize method | Resampling used when scaling the bitmap.\nuse: Scanned heights, hand-painted stamps, reference photos.\ncop: @file (SideFX lists File for loading images): {File Name}, {Incoming Colorspace} (Raw for data such as heights), {Border}, and {Channel Type}=Mono for grayscale.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-svg\nname: SVG\nsrc: atomic-nodes/svg/svg.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Renders an SVG vector image as a bitmap.\nk: Color mode | Colour or grayscale output.\nk: Background color | Background value where no vector shape is drawn.\nk: PKG resource path | Reference to the SVG resource.\nuse: Logos, ornaments and sharp-edged vector stamps for heights.\ncop: Rasterise the vector outside Houdini and load it with @file, or rebuild the shapes with @curve3d and @rasterizecurves / @maskfromcurves (curves rasterised into a layer). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-text\nname: Text\nsrc: atomic-nodes/text/text.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Places user text with font, size, alignment and transformation.\nk: Color mode | Colour or grayscale.\nk: Text | The text string.\nk: Font | Font resource.\nk: Font size | Size in points.\nk: Alignment | Left, centre or right.\nk: Position | Position of the text.\nuse: Engraved or embossed lettering in heights.\ncop: @font ({Text}, {Font}, {Font Size}, {Horizontal Alignment}, {Output Type}=Mask or SDF).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-channel-shuffle\nname: Channels Shuffle\nsrc: atomic-nodes/channel-shuffle/channel-shuffle.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Rearranges the colour channels of one or two inputs into the output (pack and swap channels in any combination).\nk: Red channel | Source channel for the output red.\nk: Green channel | Source channel for the output green.\nk: Blue channel | Source channel for the output blue.\nk: Alpha channel | Source channel for the output alpha.\nuse: Channel packing for engines, DirectX versus OpenGL flips, extracting a height from a packed map.\ncop: @channelswap: {Source for Red}, {Source for Green}, {Source for Blue}, {Source for Alpha} choose Red, Green, Blue, Alpha, Zero, One or Value; the second input is the {i:alternate} input.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-pixel-processor\nname: Pixel Processor\nsrc: atomic-nodes/pixel-processor/pixel-processor.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Runs a Substance function graph for every output pixel (optionally sampling input images), the most versatile node able to perform any per-pixel mathematics.\nkx: Color mode | Boolean | Grayscale or colour output.\nkx: Per pixel function | Float or Float4 | The function graph evaluated per pixel (the $pos variable gives the normalised pixel position).\nuse: Custom filters and generators in height graphs: custom noises, cellular automata passes, slope-based erosion approximations, anything not covered by other nodes.\ncop: @wrangle (VEX per pixel, with {Type}=Mono and the inputs as bindings), or @opencl for GPU kernels, or @pythonsnippet (slower); the function graph nodes translate to VEX operations. The Houdini equivalent of sampling input images is the bindings and the VEX texture functions.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-value-processor\nname: Value Processor\nsrc: atomic-nodes/value-processor/value-processor.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Evaluates a function graph once and outputs a single value usable elsewhere in the graph (no per-pixel work).\nkx: Value processor function | any value type | The function graph evaluated to compute the output value.\nuse: Computing graph-level numbers (such as a threshold from image statistics) and feeding them to parameters.\ncop: @statistics for the image-derived numbers plus Houdini parameter expressions (channel references and Python or HScript expressions) for the arithmetic.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-fx-map\nname: FX-Map\nsrc: atomic-nodes/fx-map/fx-map.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Replicates and subdivides an input pattern repeatedly under function graphs that control each placement (quadrant subdivision, iteration, per-pattern transforms); the most complex atomic node.\nkx: Color mode | Boolean | Grayscale or colour (colour is slower).\nkx: Background | Float or Float4 | Background value onto which patterns are composited.\nkx: Render region | Float4 | Starting pixel range for each side (stretches the result).\nkx: Tiling region | Float4 | Offset of the tiling distance.\nkx: Cull outside | Boolean | Optimisation that culls patterns outside the normal range.\nkx: Roughness | Float | Depth and opacity multiplier biasing the FX-Map blending.\nkx: Global opacity | Float | Global opacity of the output.\nuse: Custom scatter patterns (recursive cracks, quadtree tilings, Voronoi-like layouts, procedural leaf and rock distributions) that tile generators cannot express.\ncop: No single node. Generate the points in SOPs (or with @pointgenerate and @configurestamps), then stamp patterns with @stamppoint or @shapescatter ({Blend Method}=Maximum), and iterate with @copyxform; recursive subdivision logic is written in VEX or Python (@wrangle, @pythonsnippet). UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-input-color\nname: Input Color\nsrc: atomic-nodes/input-color/input-color.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Creates a colour input slot for a graph used inside another graph, with an optional default value.\nk: PKG resource path | Preview bitmap for the input.\nk: Default value | Value used when nothing is connected.\nuse: Building reusable height tools whose input is a colour image.\ncop: The @input node inside a subnetwork or HDA (inputs are typed by the layer connected); a default is set with @switchifwired.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-input-grayscale\nname: Input Grayscale\nsrc: atomic-nodes/input-grayscale/input-grayscale.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Creates a grayscale input slot for a graph, with an optional default value; input attributes (identifier, label, usage) describe the slot.\nk: PKG resource path | Preview bitmap for the input.\nk: Default value | Value used when nothing is connected.\nuse: Reusable height filters that take a mask or height as input.\ncop: @input in a COP subnetwork or HDA, with @switchifwired / @twowayswitchifwired to supply a default when unconnected.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-input-value\nname: Input Value\nsrc: atomic-nodes/input-value/input-value.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Creates a value (number or vector) input slot for a graph.\nk: Default value | Value used when nothing is connected.\nuse: Passing parameters into reusable sub-graphs.\ncop: A promoted parameter on the HDA (Edit Parameter Interface), referenced by channel expressions.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-output\nname: Output\nsrc: atomic-nodes/output/output.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Defines the result of a graph; its attributes (identifier, label, usage, group) label the output and drive how integrations use it. A graph needs at least one.\nnote: The page documents attributes (Identifier, Description, Label, User data, Group, Usage, Color space) and no parameters.\nuse: Declaring height, normal, AO and basecolor outputs with usages that the 3D view and exporters understand.\ncop: @null with a descriptive name plus @rop_image for file export (SideFX-confirmed); inside HDAs the @output node collects the outputs.\nstatus: sidefx\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3834,"cache_read_input_tokens":699898,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3834,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_016mRWbhaczz8C5vqZMUHE2R":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/08_atomic.txt","content":"## atomic-blend\nname: Blend\nsrc: atomic-nodes/blend/blend.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Combines a foreground and a background with a blending mode and an optional opacity mask; the most useful atomic node, comparable to two stacked layers with a blend mode in Photoshop or Painter.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground, multiplied with the Opacity input.\nk: Blending mode | Blend operation: Copy, Add (linear dodge), Subtract, Multiply, Add Sub, Max (lighten), Min (darken), Switch, Divide, Overlay, Screen, Soft light.\nk: Alpha blending | How alpha is treated for colour inputs (use source alpha, ignore alpha, straight or premultiplied blending).\nk: Cropping area | Crop region that acts like an extra opacity mask.\nuse: Everything in a height graph: Add or Max to stack bumps, Subtract to carve, Min to cut, Multiply to mask, Copy with a mask to composite two heights; both inputs must be of the same type (grayscale or colour).\ncop: @blend. SD mode to COP {Mode}: Copy is Over (or Blend with the mask), Add is Add, Subtract is Subtract (check operand order, or use {Swap FG and BG}), Multiply is Multiply, Max is Maximum, Min is Minimum, Divide is Divide, Overlay is Overlay, Screen is Screen, Soft light is SoftLight; Add Sub (foreground above 0.5 adds, below subtracts) has no mode: remap the foreground from 0..1 to -1..1 with @remap and use Add (UNVERIFIED). Opacity is the {Mask} amount and the {i:mask} input; Switch is @switch.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## blend-modes\nname: Blending modes (Blend node reference)\nsrc: blending-modes-description.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Reference page for the twelve modes of the Blend node: Copy, Add (linear dodge), Subtract, Multiply, Add Sub, Max (lighten), Min (darken), Switch, Divide, Overlay, Screen and Soft light.\nnote: Not a node; documented because every height graph relies on these modes. The Switch mode skips computing the unused branch, which can speed up a graph.\nuse: Choosing the right mode: Add and Subtract for additive and carving heights, Max and Min for union and intersection of shapes, Multiply for masks, Overlay and Soft light for detail contrast, Divide for flattening.\ncop: @blend {Mode} lists Blend, Over, Under, Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Screen, Hypot, Difference, Exclusion, Sharpen, Maximum, Minimum, Overlay, SoftLight, HardLight, Dodge, Burn, Hue, Saturation, Luminosity, Color; the extra COP modes Hypot, Exclusion and HardLight have no SD Blend counterpart. Use @average (Layer Merge, {Operation} Add, Average, Multiply, Min, Max, Over) to merge many layers at once.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-blur\nname: Blur\nsrc: atomic-nodes/blur/blur.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Fast, basic box blur; for anything demanding the documentation recommends Blur HQ.\nkx: Intensity | 0 to unlimited | Distance of the blur; at high values the image turns into its average colour.\nuse: Quick softening of masks or heights where quality is not critical.\ncop: @blur with {Filter}=Box.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atomic-directional-blur\nname: Directional Blur\nsrc: atomic-nodes/directional-blur/directional-blur.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Motion-blur like blur along a chosen direction; unlike Blur it works along a user-defined angle.\nk: Intensity | Blur radius in pixels.\nk: Angle | Direction in turns, clockwise from horizontal.\nuse: Brushed metal, wind streaks, softening edges along one axis, building anisotropic grain from noise.\ncop: @streakblur (SideFX-confirmed): {Direction Type}=Angle, {Angle} in degrees (SD uses turns, multiply by 360), {Length} for Intensity, {Units}=Pixels, {Mode}=Blur.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-directional-warp\nname: Directional Warp\nsrc: atomic-nodes/directional-warp/directional-warp.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Displaces pixels in one chosen direction, scaled per pixel by an intensity map, which deforms the input.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the warp.\nk: Warp angle | Direction of the warp, in turns.\nk: Input filtering mode | Nearest or bilinear sampling of the input.\nk: Intensity map offset | Value subtracted from the intensity input.\nuse: Pushing shapes sideways by a mask, wobbling edges, streaking, the engine inside Slope Blur and Multi Directional Warp.\ncop: @distort (SideFX lists the Warp node under Distort): {Direction Type}=Angle with {Angle}, {Scale} for Intensity, the intensity map on the {i:scale} input (which multiplies {Scale}); subtract the map offset beforehand with @bright {Shift}. {Filter} chooses the sampling.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-warp\nname: Warp\nsrc: atomic-nodes/warp/warp.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Displaces pixels according to the slopes of a separate gradient input, pushing uniformly away from white areas.\nk: Intensity | Strength of the warp.\nk: Input filtering mode | Nearest or bilinear sampling of the input.\nuse: Distorting patterns by a height map (organic edges, cracked lines following a noise), the most common distortion of tiles and cracks.\ncop: @distort (SideFX-confirmed) with the Distort by Slope recipe: @slopedir on the gradient map feeds the {i:dir} input; the help describes the result as pushing away from larger values toward lower ones, which matches the SD behaviour. {Scale} is Intensity, {Streak}=Off.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## atomic-distance\nname: Distance\nsrc: atomic-nodes/distance/distance.md\ncat: Atomic nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Finds the nearest white pixel of a mask and outputs a linear gradient fading from it (or the colour of the nearest pixel from a source image).\nk: Color mode | Grayscale or colour output.\nk: Maximum distance | Maximum distance searched, in pixels.\nk: Combine source/distance | How the optional source is combined with the distance fade.\nk: Distance mode | Euclidean, Manhattan or Chebyshev metric.\nuse: Bevels, rounded edges, joint gradients, rust and dirt halos from a binary mask; the common recipe is Distance then Histogram Scan.\ncop: SideFX-confirmed: @feather and @extrapolateboundaries (th