{"parentUuid":"732f93bf-3165-400b-ab67-2d03ad65d7d8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfve9WojpDLLm7Fj9b5dz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01W7YWMnpfxEE8PhwtNtPzUf","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/09_material_filters.txt","content":"## height-blend\nname: Height Blend\nsrc: /effects-material/height-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: high\npurpose: Combines two height maps according to their heights (the higher surface wins) and returns the blended height plus a black and white mask that can be used elsewhere; the height-only counterpart of Material Height Blend.\nk: Height Offset | Offsets the heights so the blend level moves along the height axis; the main control.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the transition between the two heights.\nk: Mode | Balanced height or bottom height priority.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground height.\nuse: Blending two terrain or material heights so that peaks of one poke through the other (sand over rock, moss in the valleys of stone, dirt layers between pebbles); the returned mask drives colour and roughness blends.\ncop: No single node; the basic chain uses @compareblend (new in COP 22): {i:A} = top height plus the offset (add with @bright {Shift}), {i:B} = bottom height, {i:true} = top, {i:false} = bottom, {True If}=A greater than B (>), with {Falloff}=Linear and {Tolerance} acting as the contrast softness. The mask is the same comparison made with @compare. Alternative: @blend {Mode}=Subtract of the two heights, then @remap {Operation}=Threshold with {Width} for the contrast, used as the {i:mask} of a final @blend. UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-height-blend\nname: Material Height Blend\nsrc: /effects-material/material-height-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: high\npurpose: Blends two full materials using their own height maps (no user mask); at least one height must not be uniform.\nk: Channels | Which material channels are processed.\nk: Height Offset | Moves the blend level along the height axis.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the transition.\nk: Mode | Balanced height or bottom height priority.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground material.\nk: Albedo Matching | Amount of colour matching between albedos.\nuse: Combining two scanned or procedural materials where heights decide who covers whom.\ncop: The Height Blend chain above builds a mask once; apply that mask to every channel of the two materials with @blend (use @cablepack / @cableunpack to carry the channels as a cable), and renormalise the blended normal with @convertnormal {Normalize}. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-blend\nname: Material Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: medium\npurpose: Multi-channel counterpart of the atomic Blend node: blends two full materials with a grayscale mask or a single colour from an ID mask, with an opacity and blending mode per channel.\nk: Channels | Which material channels are blended.\nk: Opacity | Per-channel opacity between foreground and background.\nk: Blending Mode | Per-channel blend mode (Normal, Add, Subtract, Multiply, Add/Sub, Max, Min, Switch).\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses one colour of an ID map as the mask.\nk: Fuzziness | Spread of the picked colour into neighbours.\nk: Padding | Contrast of the colour mask transition.\nuse: Mixing two materials such as dirt over metal through a shared mask.\ncop: One @blend per channel (heights with {Mode}=Maximum, colours with Blend / Over, normals with @combinenormals or a masked @blend plus renormalisation), the same mask wired into each; channels carried by a cable (@cablepack, @cableunpack). @idtomask replaces the colour ID mask.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-adjustment-blend\nname: Material Adjustment Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-adjustment-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Adjusts any channels of a full material through a mask (for example brighter diffuse and darker roughness in the same area).\nk: Channels | Which channels are adjusted.\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses a colour ID mask instead of a grayscale mask.\nk: Fuzziness | Spread of the colour selection.\nk: Padding | Contrast of the colour masking.\nuse: Masked touch-ups of a whole material.\ncop: Per channel @remap or @colorcorrect / @bright / @contrast blended through the mask with @blend.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-color-blend\nname: Material Color Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-color-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Blends solid colours on top of channels of a full material (blend-type adjustments rather than levels-type).\nk: Channels | Which channels receive the colour.\nk: Source | For the normal channel, whether the source is a height or a mask.\nk: Blending Mode | Per-channel blend mode; for normals Combine or Blend.\nk: Height Intensity | Intensity of the height used for normals.\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses a colour ID mask.\nuse: Tinting and flat overrides on parts of a material.\ncop: @constant layers blended with @blend {Mode} per channel through the mask.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-switch\nname: Material Switch\nsrc: /blending-material/material-switch/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Multi-channel version of Switch: returns one of two materials depending on a boolean.\nk: Channels | Which channels are switched.\nk: Switch | Chooses material 1 or 2.\nuse: Variants of a material in a graph.\ncop: @cableswitch (selects an input cable) when the materials travel as cables, or @switch per layer.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-material-blend\nname: Multi-Material Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/multi-material-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Combines up to 16 full materials according to a Material ID or Colour ID map baked from a mesh.\nk: Materials | Number of materials blended (2 to 16).\nk: Channels | Channels processed.\nk: Color | ID colour matching a material slot.\nk: Fuzziness | Bleed into neighbouring colours.\nk: Padding | Hardness of transitions.\nuse: Texturing a whole prop with several materials from a baked ID map.\ncop: @idtomask for each ID colour (or @segmentbyvalue) and one @blend (or @cableswitch) per material and channel.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## base-material\nname: Base Material\nsrc: /pbr-utilities/base-material/\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Quickest way to create a multi-channel material from solid colours and values (placeholder or starting point), with metal and specular presets and optional user maps.\nk: PBR Workflow | Metal / roughness or specular / glossiness.\nk: Material Preset | Quick pure metal presets or dielectric.\nk: Base Color | Solid base colour.\nk: Roughness Value | Base roughness value.\nk: Grunge Amount | How much an optional grunge map modulates roughness or gloss.\nk: Normal from Height Intensity | Converts a custom height to the normal output.\nk: Height Position | Solid height value.\nuse: Placeholder materials in a graph.\ncop: @previewmaterial (SideFX-confirmed correspondence of Base Material) fed by @constant layers; or @usdmaterial for a MaterialX / USD preview surface.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## basecolor-metallic-roughness-converter\nname: BaseColor Metallic Roughness converter\nsrc: basecolor-metallic-roughness-converter.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Converts base colour, metallic and roughness maps to other PBR outputs such as specular / glossiness, V-Ray, Corona, Redshift, Arnold or RenderMan.\nk: Use SpecularLevel input | Exposes a specular level input used in the conversion.\nk: Target | Target PBR model.\nuse: Exporting to a renderer with a different PBR parametrisation.\ncop: Per-channel arithmetic in @wrangle (diffuse equals base colour times one minus metallic, specular equals a lerp of 0.04 and base colour by metallic, glossiness equals one minus roughness; standard formulas, UNVERIFIED for each target).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-albedo-safe-color\nname: PBR Albedo Safe Color\nsrc: pbr-albedo-safe-color.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Corrects base colour or diffuse values that fall outside the physically plausible range.\nk: PBR Workflow | Base Color / Metallic or Diffuse / Specular.\nk: Tolerance | Tolerance for out-of-range values.\nuse: Keeping albedo in a valid range.\ncop: @remap or @clamp ({Lower Limit}, {Upper Limit}) on the albedo luminance, applied through @hsv {Value Scale}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-basecolor-metallic-validate\nname: PBR BaseColor Metallic Validate\nsrc: pbr-basecolor-metallic-validate.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Produces a good to bad heat map showing where base colour and metallic values break PBR rules.\nk: Validation Mode | Albedo, metal or combined check.\nk: Albedo Dark Range Threshold | Lower albedo limit (50 or 30 sRGB).\nk: Metal Reflectance Range | Accepted metal reflectance range.\nk: Overlay Map | Overlays the inputs for debugging.\nuse: Debugging albedo and metallic maps.\ncop: @compare / @remap against the allowed ranges, coloured with @monotorgb.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-dielectric-f0\nname: PBR Dielectric F0\nsrc: pbr-dielectric-f0.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Preset helper that outputs correct specular F0 values for common dielectrics.\nk: Specular F0 | Material preset or custom IOR.\nk: Specular Range | Adjusts the value within the preset range.\nk: IOR | Custom index of refraction.\nuse: Picking a physically correct specular value.\ncop: @constant with F0 computed from the IOR: ((IOR minus 1) divided by (IOR plus 1)) squared (standard Fresnel formula).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-metal-reflectance\nname: PBR Metal Reflectance\nsrc: pbr-metal-reflectance.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Preset helper returning the reflectance colours of a few pure metals.\nk: Metal Color | Gold, silver, aluminium, iron, copper, titanium, nickel, cobalt or platinum.\nuse: Base colour for pure metals.\ncop: @constant with an RGB colour taken from a metal reflectance table (values must be entered by hand).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-render\nname: PBR Render\nsrc: /pbr-utilities/pbr-render/\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: medium\npurpose: Renders a PBR material on a sphere, plane or cylinder with image based lighting, as a texture inside the graph (thumbnails, previews, 2D assets).\nk: Shape | Sphere, plane or cylinder.\nk: Displacement Intensity | Height displacement strength.\nk: Environment Rotation | Rotates the lighting environment.\nk: Exposure | Camera exposure.\nk: Tone Mapper | Linear, ACES or Filmic Hejl.\nk: Diffuse Quality | Sample count for the diffuse term.\nuse: Previewing a height-driven material with lighting during graph development.\ncop: @previewmaterial on test geometry (@testgeometry_shaderball) displayed in the Scene Viewer or rendered with Karma; inside COP the fake lighting alternative is @light with @heighttonormal and @heighttoshadow. @tonemap provides the tone mapping curve.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-render-mapping\nname: PBR Render Mapping\nsrc: pbr-render-mapping.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Extension of PBR Render that maps another texture back onto the rendered shape using its UV output, to build channel breakdown images.\nk: Background Color | Solid background colour.\nuse: Preview sheets of every channel on the same shape.\ncop: @sample (UV Sample) with the UV output of the render; @contactsheet to lay out the channel breakdown.\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## season-filter\nname: Season Filter\nsrc: /effects-material/season-filter/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: low\npurpose: Legacy all-in-one filter adding water, ice, snow and moss to a material (not PBR-correct; kept for compatibility).\nk: Effect From Height or Normal | Which map drives the effects.\nk: Water Level | Raises or lowers the water level.\nk: Snow | Snow coverage.\nk: Moss | Moss coverage.\nk: Moss Scale | Scale of the generated moss.\nk: Ice | How frozen the water is.\nuse: Quick seasonal variants; the newer Snow Cover and Water Level are recommended.\ncop: Compose from heights: a water mask with @remap {Operation}=Threshold on the height, snow mask from the upward-facing normal (@channelextract of the blue channel) and moss from noise masked by AO, blended with @blend.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## snow-cover\nname: Snow Cover\nsrc: /effects-material/snow-cover/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: medium\npurpose: PBR-correct snow buildup on a full material; relies on a good height map (for example from a photoscan).\nk: Fresh Snow | Amount of snow on raised areas.\nk: Melted Snow | Amount of melted snow in lowered corners.\nk: Buildup | Height pile-up effect.\nk: Smoothness | Smoothing of height details by the snow.\nk: Flakes Intensity | Flake detail in the normal map.\nuse: Snow accumulation on rocks, roofs and ground heights; the height output adds the snow layer to the surface.\ncop: Snow mask from slope and curvature (@slopedir magnitude or @heighttonormal blue channel, @curvature convexity), thickened with @feather, and the snow height added to the base with @blend {Mode}=Add; flakes from @randommono. Chain is UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## water-level\nname: Water Level\nsrc: /effects-material/water-level/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: medium\npurpose: PBR-correct water level applied to a full material with a good height map: wet edges, depth blur, sludge and frost.\nk: Water Level | Main control raising or lowering the water.\nk: Water Darkness | General transparency of the water.\nk: Edges Wetness | Wet look of the water edges.\nk: Depth Blur Amount | Blur depending on depth below water.\nk: Sludge Depth | Depth at which sludge appears.\nk: Frost | Amount of frost.\nuse: Flooding the low areas of a ground height; the threshold of the height gives the shoreline mask.\ncop: @remap {Operation}=Threshold on the height at the water level for the wet mask, flat water height set with @compareblend (true input = water level constant, false input = original height), shore wetness with @feather, depth blur via @blur with the depth as {i:size}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## bitmap-to-material-light\nname: Bitmap to Material Light\nsrc: /1-click/bitmap-to-material-light/\ncat: Material filters > 1-click\nrel: low\npurpose: Converts a single diffuse or base colour image into a full material (simple version of the commercial Bitmap2Material).\nk: Depth Balance | Bias or shift of the height map.\nk: Normal Strength | Strength of the generated normal map.\nk: Relief Equalizer | Balance between detail scales in the conversion.\nk: Pinch Intensity | Sharpens normal transitions.\nk: Ambient Occlusion Spread | Spread of the generated AO.\nuse: Fast height and normal estimates from a photo.\ncop: @mono (luminance) then @filterfrequencies / @equalize to build a height proxy, @heighttonormal for the normal and @heighttoambientocclusion for AO. Quality differs and the mapping is UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-transform\nname: Material Transform\nsrc: /transforms-material/material-transform/\ncat: Material filters > Transforms\nrel: low\npurpose: Multi-channel version of Transformation 2D: transforms every channel of a material together.\nk: Transformation | Rotation and scale matrix.\nk: Offset | Translation of the result.\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nk: Channels | Which channels are transformed.\nuse: Scaling or offsetting a whole material consistently.\ncop: @xform2d applied to each wire of the cable, with {Border}=Wrap for tiling; normals additionally rotate their vectors with @vectorxform.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## ao-cancellation\nname: AO Cancellation\nsrc: /scan-processing/ao-cancellation/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Removes baked ambient occlusion lighting from an albedo using a separate AO map.\nk: AO Cancellation | Strength of the removal.\nk: AO Saturation | Saturation compensation in the corrected areas.\nuse: Cleaning scanned base colours.\ncop: @blend {Mode}=Divide of the albedo by the AO (clamped with @clamp), then @hsv {Saturation Scale} for the compensation. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atlas-scatter\nname: Atlas Scatter\nsrc: /scan-processing/atlas-scatter/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: medium\npurpose: Splits an atlas of full-material elements and scatters them on a background like Shape Splatter, with height-aware blending.\nk: Atlas Input Resolution | Resolution used for the atlas.\nk: X Amount | Number of scatter columns.\nk: Y Amount | Number of scatter rows.\nk: Pattern Distribution Mode | Order in which atlas elements are used.\nk: Scale Random | Random scale per shape.\nk: Position Random | Random position offset.\nk: Rotation from Bg Slope | Rotation following the background slope.\nk: Blend Mode | Height Blend or Alpha Test for overlaps.\nk: Conform to Background | Deforms the shape height to follow the background.\nuse: Scattering scanned stones, leaves or debris onto a ground height.\ncop: @segmentbyconnectivity plus @uvmapbyid and @crop to extract the elements (see Atlas Splitter), then @shapescatter ({Blend Method}=Maximum) with stamps from the atlas; height conformity is manual (UNVERIFIED).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## atlas-splitter\nname: Atlas Splitter\nsrc: /scan-processing/atlas-splitter/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Detects the separate elements of an atlas (an advanced Flood Fill) and outputs each as an individual material, or rearranges them on a grid.\nk: Grid View | Displays detected shapes in a grid.\nk: Auto Scale | Scales shapes to fit the grid cell.\nk: Auto Crop | Crops to the largest shape.\nk: Shape Selection | Which shape is highlighted or returned.\nk: Ignore Shape Smaller Than | Ignores small shapes.\nk: Auto Rotation | Rotates shapes by their bounding box ratio.\nk: Dilation Width | Dilation of the shapes from the opacity mask.\nuse: Preparing scanned elements for scattering.\ncop: @segmentbyconnectivity on the opacity, @uvmapbyid ({Mode}=Minimum, Maximum, Size, Center) for each shape bounds, then @crop with its {i:bounds} input or @boundrect.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## clone-patch\nname: Clone Patch\nsrc: /scan-processing/clone-patch/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: medium\npurpose: Procedural, non-destructive clone stamp that copies one area of the input to another, with edge blending analysis.\nk: Is Normal (only for Color) | Treats the input as a normal map for blending.\nk: Shape | Stamp shape (square or disc).\nk: Threshold | How far the blended area reaches.\nk: Blur | Softness of the stamp edge.\nk: Smoothness | Rounding of the stamp outline.\nk: Grid Resolution | Quality of the blending analysis.\nk: Source Offset | Translates the source area.\nk: Target Offset | Translates the target area.\nuse: Removing unwanted detail from scanned heights.\ncop: @xform2d to move the source area and @blend with a feathered mask; seamless blending with @solvepoisson (solves Poisson's equation inside a 2D boundary, the principle of gradient-domain cloning). UNVERIFIED chain.\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-clone-patch\nname: Material Clone Patch\nsrc: /scan-processing/material-clone-patch/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Clone Patch applied to every channel of a full material at once.\nk: Channels | Channels processed.\nk: Shape | Stamp shape.\nk: Threshold (for multiple channels) | How far the blended area reaches.\nk: Blur | Edge softness.\nuse: Removing a detail from all material channels.\ncop: Clone chain of Clone Patch applied to each wire of a cable.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-clone-patch\nname: Multi Clone Patch\nsrc: /scan-processing/multi-clone-patch/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Applies the same Clone Patch operation to up to eight inputs, for multi-angle photographs.\nk: Input Count | Number of inputs receiving the same patch.\nk: Shape | Stamp shape.\nk: Threshold | How far the blend reaches.\nuse: Cleaning a photo set before multi-angle processing.\ncop: The same clone chain duplicated per input (a subnetwork or HDA with {Input Count}).\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## color-equalizer\nname: Color Equalizer\nsrc: /scan-processing/color-equalizer/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: High-quality highpass for colour differences; evens out colour and removes unwanted tints at a chosen scale without desaturating.\nk: Input Tiled | Preserves tiling at the edges.\nk: Radius | Equalising radius (larger removes only large colour differences).\nk: Bright/Dark Balance | Bias towards removing or keeping darker tints.\nk: Custom Color Variation | Varies the effect towards a chosen colour.\nk: Mask Source | Source of an optional mask.\nuse: De-tinting photo scans before extracting heights.\ncop: @blur with a large {Size} and {Border}=Wrap, then @blend {Mode}=Divide or Subtract against the original on the chroma only (@hsv).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-color-equalizer\nname: Multi Color Equalizer\nsrc: /scan-processing/multi-color-equalizer/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Multi-input version of Color Equalizer for multi-angle photographs.\nk: Input Count | Number of inputs processed in parallel.\nk: Radius | Equalising radius.\nk: Bright/Dark Balance | Bias for dark tints.\nuse: Preparing multi-angle photo sets.\ncop: Duplicate the Color Equalizer chain per input.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## crop\nname: Crop\nsrc: /scan-processing/crop/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: medium\npurpose: Parametric, non-destructive crop that keeps a chosen area and discards the rest.\nk: Input Size | Input resolution and proportions (important for non-square images).\nk: Background | Fill value outside the crop.\nk: Transform | Rotation and scale of the crop.\nk: Offset | Translation of the crop.\nk: Is Normal (only for Color version) | Treats the input as a normal map.\nuse: Extracting a tile or a detail from a scan.\ncop: @crop ({Method}=Lower Left and Size, {Units}, {Mode}=Reframe to Crop) or @xform2d plus @resample.\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-crop\nname: Material Crop\nsrc: /scan-processing/material-crop/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Crop applied to all channels of a full material in parallel.\nk: Channels | Channels cropped.\nk: Input Size | Input resolution.\nk: Transform | Rotation and scale of the crop.\nk: Offset | Translation.\nuse: Cropping whole scanned materials.\ncop: @crop on each wire of the cable with identical bounds.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-crop\nname: Multi Crop\nsrc: /scan-processing/multi-crop/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Crop applied to several inputs at once, for multi-angle photos.\nk: Input Count | Number of inputs.\nk: Input Size | Input resolution.\nk: Transform | Rotation and scale of the crop.\nk: Offset | Translation.\nuse: Aligned crops across a photo set.\ncop: @crop with shared bounds duplicated per input.\ngray: Grayscale version.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-angle-to-albedo\nname: Multi-Angle to Albedo\nsrc: /scan-processing/multi-angle-to-albedo/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Removes lighting from a set of photographs taken under different lighting angles and combines them into one lighting-neutral albedo.\nk: Samples Amount | Number of input samples (2 to 8).\nuse: Building albedo from a photographed set.\ncop: No direct node. A per-pixel median or minimum of the aligned inputs with @average ({Operation}=Min) or @median, then colour clean-up; UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-angle-to-normal\nname: Multi-Angle to Normal\nsrc: /scan-processing/multi-angle-to-normal/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: low\npurpose: Builds an accurate normal map from photographs lit from different angles (photometric reconstruction).\nk: Normal Format | DirectX or OpenGL.\nk: Samples Amount | Number of samples (2 to 8).\nk: Intensity | Strength of the normal map.\nk: First Sample Light Angle | Light direction of the first input.\nk: Next Sample Light Angle | Direction in which the light moves between samples.\nuse: Scanning surface relief with a handheld light.\ncop: No direct node. Photometric stereo solved per pixel in @wrangle or @opencl (least squares over the known light directions). UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## smart-auto-tile\nname: Smart Auto Tile\nsrc: /scan-processing/smart-auto-tile/\ncat: Material filters > Scan processing\nrel: medium\npurpose: Turns a non-tiling set of base colour, normal and height maps into tiling versions using an analysis of all channels to find the best blending seam.\nk: Input Size | Input resolution and proportions.\nk: Detect Edges | Edge-aware blending.\nk: Threshold | Edge threshold (global or per channel).\nk: Cut Offset | Position of the cut along X and Y.\nk: Blur | Blur of the blend transition.\nk: Smoothness | Jaggedness of the edge analysis.\nk: Grid Resolution | Quality of the edge analysis.\nuse: Making scanned heights tile seamlessly.\ncop: Offset by half with @xform2d {Border}=Wrap, then cross-fade the seam using a seam mask built from @edgedetect, @feather and @blend; the ID-aware cut search is not available. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4644,"cache_read_input_tokens":741414,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4644,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01W7YWMnpfxEE8PhwtNtPzUf":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/09_material_filters.txt","content":"## height-blend\nname: Height Blend\nsrc: /effects-material/height-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: high\npurpose: Combines two height maps according to their heights (the higher surface wins) and returns the blended height plus a black and white mask that can be used elsewhere; the height-only counterpart of Material Height Blend.\nk: Height Offset | Offsets the heights so the blend level moves along the height axis; the main control.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the transition between the two heights.\nk: Mode | Balanced height or bottom height priority.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground height.\nuse: Blending two terrain or material heights so that peaks of one poke through the other (sand over rock, moss in the valleys of stone, dirt layers between pebbles); the returned mask drives colour and roughness blends.\ncop: No single node; the basic chain uses @compareblend (new in COP 22): {i:A} = top height plus the offset (add with @bright {Shift}), {i:B} = bottom height, {i:true} = top, {i:false} = bottom, {True If}=A greater than B (>), with {Falloff}=Linear and {Tolerance} acting as the contrast softness. The mask is the same comparison made with @compare. Alternative: @blend {Mode}=Subtract of the two heights, then @remap {Operation}=Threshold with {Width} for the contrast, used as the {i:mask} of a final @blend. UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-height-blend\nname: Material Height Blend\nsrc: /effects-material/material-height-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Effects\nrel: high\npurpose: Blends two full materials using their own height maps (no user mask); at least one height must not be uniform.\nk: Channels | Which material channels are processed.\nk: Height Offset | Moves the blend level along the height axis.\nk: Contrast | Sharpness of the transition.\nk: Mode | Balanced height or bottom height priority.\nk: Opacity | Opacity of the foreground material.\nk: Albedo Matching | Amount of colour matching between albedos.\nuse: Combining two scanned or procedural materials where heights decide who covers whom.\ncop: The Height Blend chain above builds a mask once; apply that mask to every channel of the two materials with @blend (use @cablepack / @cableunpack to carry the channels as a cable), and renormalise the blended normal with @convertnormal {Normalize}. UNVERIFIED.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-blend\nname: Material Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: medium\npurpose: Multi-channel counterpart of the atomic Blend node: blends two full materials with a grayscale mask or a single colour from an ID mask, with an opacity and blending mode per channel.\nk: Channels | Which material channels are blended.\nk: Opacity | Per-channel opacity between foreground and background.\nk: Blending Mode | Per-channel blend mode (Normal, Add, Subtract, Multiply, Add/Sub, Max, Min, Switch).\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses one colour of an ID map as the mask.\nk: Fuzziness | Spread of the picked colour into neighbours.\nk: Padding | Contrast of the colour mask transition.\nuse: Mixing two materials such as dirt over metal through a shared mask.\ncop: One @blend per channel (heights with {Mode}=Maximum, colours with Blend / Over, normals with @combinenormals or a masked @blend plus renormalisation), the same mask wired into each; channels carried by a cable (@cablepack, @cableunpack). @idtomask replaces the colour ID mask.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-adjustment-blend\nname: Material Adjustment Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-adjustment-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Adjusts any channels of a full material through a mask (for example brighter diffuse and darker roughness in the same area).\nk: Channels | Which channels are adjusted.\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses a colour ID mask instead of a grayscale mask.\nk: Fuzziness | Spread of the colour selection.\nk: Padding | Contrast of the colour masking.\nuse: Masked touch-ups of a whole material.\ncop: Per channel @remap or @colorcorrect / @bright / @contrast blended through the mask with @blend.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-color-blend\nname: Material Color Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/material-color-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Blends solid colours on top of channels of a full material (blend-type adjustments rather than levels-type).\nk: Channels | Which channels receive the colour.\nk: Source | For the normal channel, whether the source is a height or a mask.\nk: Blending Mode | Per-channel blend mode; for normals Combine or Blend.\nk: Height Intensity | Intensity of the height used for normals.\nk: Color ID Mask | Uses a colour ID mask.\nuse: Tinting and flat overrides on parts of a material.\ncop: @constant layers blended with @blend {Mode} per channel through the mask.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## material-switch\nname: Material Switch\nsrc: /blending-material/material-switch/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Multi-channel version of Switch: returns one of two materials depending on a boolean.\nk: Channels | Which channels are switched.\nk: Switch | Chooses material 1 or 2.\nuse: Variants of a material in a graph.\ncop: @cableswitch (selects an input cable) when the materials travel as cables, or @switch per layer.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## multi-material-blend\nname: Multi-Material Blend\nsrc: /blending-material/multi-material-blend/\ncat: Material filters > Blending\nrel: low\npurpose: Combines up to 16 full materials according to a Material ID or Colour ID map baked from a mesh.\nk: Materials | Number of materials blended (2 to 16).\nk: Channels | Channels processed.\nk: Color | ID colour matching a material slot.\nk: Fuzziness | Bleed into neighbouring colours.\nk: Padding | Hardness of transitions.\nuse: Texturing a whole prop with several materials from a baked ID map.\ncop: @idtomask for each ID colour (or @segmentbyvalue) and one @blend (or @cableswitch) per material and channel.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## base-material\nname: Base Material\nsrc: /pbr-utilities/base-material/\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Quickest way to create a multi-channel material from solid colours and values (placeholder or starting point), with metal and specular presets and optional user maps.\nk: PBR Workflow | Metal / roughness or specular / glossiness.\nk: Material Preset | Quick pure metal presets or dielectric.\nk: Base Color | Solid base colour.\nk: Roughness Value | Base roughness value.\nk: Grunge Amount | How much an optional grunge map modulates roughness or gloss.\nk: Normal from Height Intensity | Converts a custom height to the normal output.\nk: Height Position | Solid height value.\nuse: Placeholder materials in a graph.\ncop: @previewmaterial (SideFX-confirmed correspondence of Base Material) fed by @constant layers; or @usdmaterial for a MaterialX / USD preview surface.\nstatus: sidefx\n\n## basecolor-metallic-roughness-converter\nname: BaseColor Metallic Roughness converter\nsrc: basecolor-metallic-roughness-converter.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Converts base colour, metallic and roughness maps to other PBR outputs such as specular / glossiness, V-Ray, Corona, Redshift, Arnold or RenderMan.\nk: Use SpecularLevel input | Exposes a specular level input used in the conversion.\nk: Target | Target PBR model.\nuse: Exporting to a renderer with a different PBR parametrisation.\ncop: Per-channel arithmetic in @wrangle (diffuse equals base colour times one minus metallic, specular equals a lerp of 0.04 and base colour by metallic, glossiness equals one minus roughness; standard formulas, UNVERIFIED for each target).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-albedo-safe-color\nname: PBR Albedo Safe Color\nsrc: pbr-albedo-safe-color.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Corrects base colour or diffuse values that fall outside the physically plausible range.\nk: PBR Workflow | Base Color / Metallic or Diffuse / Specular.\nk: Tolerance | Tolerance for out-of-range values.\nuse: Keeping albedo in a valid range.\ncop: @remap or @clamp ({Lower Limit}, {Upper Limit}) on the albedo luminance, applied through @hsv {Value Scale}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-basecolor-metallic-validate\nname: PBR BaseColor Metallic Validate\nsrc: pbr-basecolor-metallic-validate.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Produces a good to bad heat map showing where base colour and metallic values break PBR rules.\nk: Validation Mode | Albedo, metal or combined check.\nk: Albedo Dark Range Threshold | Lower albedo limit (50 or 30 sRGB).\nk: Metal Reflectance Range | Accepted metal reflectance range.\nk: Overlay Map | Overlays the inputs for debugging.\nuse: Debugging albedo and metallic maps.\ncop: @compare / @remap against the allowed ranges, coloured with @monotorgb.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-dielectric-f0\nname: PBR Dielectric F0\nsrc: pbr-dielectric-f0.md\ncat: Material filters > PBR utilities\nrel: low\npurpose: Preset helper that outputs correct specular F0 values for common dielectrics.\nk: Specular F0 | Material preset or custom IOR.\nk: Specular Range | Adjusts the value within the preset range.\nk: IOR | Custom index of refraction.\nuse: Picking a physically correct specular value.\ncop: @constant with F0 computed from the IOR: ((IOR minus 1) divided by (IOR plus 1)) squared (standard Fresnel formula).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## pbr-metal-reflectance\nname: PBR Metal Reflectance\nsrc: