{"parentUuid":"9d02aa98-69b9-4d93-95ae-b5ac42f08f4e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X8zrt2ub5tNFeSyZ46KXLb","type":"tool_result","content":"#type:     node\n#context:  cop\n#internal: wrangle\n#icon:     COP/wrangle\n\n= Wrangle =\n\n\"\"\"Runs a VEX snippet to modify layer values.\"\"\"\n\nThis is a very powerful, low-level node that lets experts who are\nfamiliar with VEX tweak pixel values using code.\n\nThis node corresponds to the [Volume Wrangle\nSOP|Node:sop/volumewrangle], but automatically converts the incoming\nlayers or VDBs to bound volumes.\n\nAll of the layers and VDBs are bound into a single geometry input `0`.\nAdditional geometry thus needs to use higher input numbers.\n\n`@P` can be used to get the current world position of the processed\npixel.\n\nNOTE:\n    While VEX is considerably more accessible than OpenCL, it is still\n    encouraged to use the OpenCL COP rather than a Wrangle.  A wrangle\n    should be used where you need access to high-level geometry\n    functions.  Wrangles are CPU nodes so can considerably slow down\n    networks.\n\n== Syntax ==\n\n:include wrangle_syntax:\n\n== Reading and modifying the pixel value == (values)\n\nThe current pixel value in a layer is available as `@<<layer_name>>`.\nYou can read this variable to get the current value, and assign it to change the value.\n\nThese will have the same VEX type as the layer - so a UV layer will\nbe of type `vector2` and have a `.x` and `.y`\n\n@parameters\n\n:include pointwrangle#snippet:\n    \nVolumes to Write to:\n    #id: vex_exportlist\n    \n    Only modify layers if their names match this pattern.\n    The default pattern allows any layer to be modified.\n    You can speed up the node by only listing layers that are _actually_ modified by the snippet.\n    \n    For example, in the following snippet, only the `density` layer is modified.\n    The `temperature` layer is not modified, only read.\n    \n    {{{\n    #!vex\n    @density = @temperature;\n    }}}\n    \n    However, for obscure reasons, the node will both modify `density` and _copy_ `temperature`.\n    This uses time and memory.\n    To prevent this, you could set this parameter to `density` to prevent the node\n    from copying `temperature`.\n    Of course, this requires that you explicitly manage the list of\n    writable layers.\n\n    \n:include /vex/_enforce_prototypes:\n    \nEvaluation Node Path:\n    #id: vex_cwdpath\n\n    VEX functions like `ch()` usually evaluate with respect to this node.\n    Providing a path here can override where the path search starts\n    from.  This is useful for embedding in a digital asset where you\n    would like the top level digital asset to be the search root.\n    \n    \nExport Parameters:\n    #id: vex_outputmask\n    \n    This pattern can be used to override\n    the export option on the VEX shader to avoid writing to \n    certain volumes.  The pattern matches the VEX parameter, not\n    the bound volume.  The volume will still be bound for\n    reading.\n\n:include /nodes/cop/_parameter_descriptions#cablerenamebylabel/:\n\n3:#internal: opencl\n4:#icon:     COP/opencl\n#type:     node\n#context:  cop\n#internal: opencl\n#icon:     COP/opencl\n\n= OpenCL =\n\n\"\"\"Executes an OpenCL kernel on layers, attributes, volumes, and VDBs.\"\"\"\n\nThe OpenCL COP provides a general interface to create and run OpenCL\nkernels on layers, attributes, volumes, and VDBs. It allows binding of constants, attributes,\nand volume data to OpenCL parameters in the kernel.\n\nWhile this node is harder to use than the [Wrangle COP|Node:/cop/wrangle], it is very\nimportant to keep all computations on the GPU for COPs, so it is\nencouraged over using VEX.\n\n== Syntax ==\n\nSee [OpenCL for VEX users|/vex/ocl] for basic information on the\nsyntax available.\n\nNOTE:\n    You likely do not need to manually edit the Signature or Bindings tabs if you are not doing something esoteric. They are often set up with the \"Create inputs and spare parameters\" button.\n\n== Warnings ==\n\n* This node requires that you understand OpenCL, as it's easy to write incorrect code using this node.\n* You can't bind geometry as a layer, you must refer to it by name in an attribute or volume binding.\n* If the `@P` [global binding|/vex/ocl#global-bindings] exists, you can't use `#bind point P port=geo float3` since there's a duplicate `P` binding. Instead, you must use `#bind point geoP name=P port=geo float3` and use `@geoP` to access the `P` attribute. \n    `@P` isn't bound when the kernel is running over attributes, so in this scenario you can use the following:\n    {{{\n    #runover attribute\n    #bind point &P port=geo float3\n    }}}\n\n@parameters\n\n== Kernel ==","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"70213ea2-72ba-4bc6-953d-214b1f6c302e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:01:33.373Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"3a2a70a9-572d-4688-827c-cbea6243b536","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9d02aa98-69b9-4d93-95ae-b5ac42f08f4e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
