{"parentUuid":"3d79c1f5-fc4f-456e-9326-6e38fe6cd9c2","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfvek65BhvzgXXJ88pCjv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y5gCpbWtA78qSNxgvW3Dyn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/work/entries/13_function_graphs.txt","content":"## fn-sample-gray\nname: Sample Gray (function node)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/sampler-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Reads the luminance of a grayscale input image at a Float2 position (0 to 1 from the top left corner) inside a Pixel Processor or FX-Map function and returns it as a Float.\nnote: Documented on the sampler nodes page together with Sample Color. Parameters: Input image (numbered from 0), Filtering mode (Nearest or Bilinear) and Addressing mode (Repeat or Clamp to Edge).\nuse: The basis of custom filters written in a Pixel Processor, for example offsets, blurs, gradient or slope estimates, and masks that read a height map at shifted positions.\ncop: @wrangle (CPU VEX, the current pixel value of a layer is read as @layername) or the recommended GPU route @opencl; random-access reads at computed positions use the sampling functions of VEX/OpenCL or an explicit UV layer with @sample (UV Sample, {Filter}, {UV Space}).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-sample-color\nname: Sample Color (function node)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/sampler-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Reads the RGBA value of a colour input image at a Float2 position and returns it as a Float4 (R, G, B, A mapped to X, Y, Z, W).\nuse: Per-pixel colour processing, packed maps, normal map manipulation in a Pixel Processor.\ncop: Same as Sample Gray with an RGBA layer: @wrangle / @opencl, or @sample for UV lookups.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-get-nodes\nname: Get nodes (Get Float, Get Float2, Get Integer and other typed Get nodes)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/get-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Typed nodes that read a variable of the current scope (an exposed parameter, a variable created with Set, or a built-in variable such as $pos, $size, $tiling, $pixelratio, $physicalsize, $depth, $number, $time).\nnote: Built-in variables documented by Adobe: $pos is the evaluated pixel position in a Pixel Processor (and the birth position of a pattern in an FX-Map), $size is the node size in pixels, $sizelog2 the same as powers of two, $pixelratio is 0 for stretch and 1 for square, $tiling is 0 to 3 for none, horizontal, vertical, both, $physicalsize is the graph physical size, $depth is the FX-Map octave, $depthpow2 is 1 over 2 to the power of the octave, $number is the index of the drawn pattern, $time is the engine time in seconds.\nuse: Reading the current pixel position, the image size and tiling mode inside Pixel Processor and FX-Map functions, and reading exposed parameters in parameter functions.\ncop: In a Wrangle COP the pixel position is available as @P (world position of the processed pixel) and the image size through the layer metadata functions; in OpenCL the equivalent globals are bound by name. Exposed parameters become HDA parameters read with ch() style expressions.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-set-sequence\nname: Set and Sequence nodes\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/fx-maps/using-substance-function-graphs-in-fx-maps/using-the-set-sequence-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: The Set node creates a named variable from its input value (visible in the parent scope), and the Sequence node forces one branch of a function to be evaluated entirely before another so the variable is ready when read.\nuse: Reusing one computed value (for example a random angle) in several parameters of an FX-Map node, and controlling the order of side effects.\ncop: Local variables in VEX or OpenCL code; evaluation order is simply the statement order.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-control-nodes\nname: If...Else, Sequence and While Loop (control nodes)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/control-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Control flow in function graphs: If...Else filters the result by a condition, Sequence computes one portion of the graph before another, While Loop runs an initial branch once and then repeats the condition and body branches until the condition returns true (with an implicit maximum iteration count).\nk: Max iterations | While Loop maximum number of iterations (-1 disables the limit).\nuse: Iterative per-pixel algorithms, conditional logic and accumulation loops in a Pixel Processor.\ncop: if, else and while statements in VEX (@wrangle) or OpenCL (@opencl); iteration over frames or passes can use the blocks of COP (@block_begin, @block_end).\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-comparison-logical\nname: Comparison and Logical nodes (Equal, Greater, Lower, And, Or, Not)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/comparison-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Comparison nodes compare the upper input to the lower one and return a boolean; Logical nodes And, Or and Not combine booleans.\nuse: Building the conditions of If...Else nodes (thresholds, ranges, masks).\ncop: Comparison and boolean operators in VEX or OpenCL; per-pixel comparisons between layers are also available without code as @compare and @compareblend.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-operator-nodes\nname: Operator nodes (Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide, Modulo)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/operator-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Classic arithmetic on node inputs; for division and modulo the divisor is the lower input, and for subtraction the subtrahend is the lower input.\nuse: Any numeric expression in a function.\ncop: Arithmetic operators in VEX or OpenCL; layer arithmetic without code is @blend (Add, Subtract, Multiply, Divide) or @function.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-function-nodes\nname: Function nodes (Pow, Sqrt, Exp, Log, Abs, Ceil, Floor, Lerp, Min, Max, Cos, Sin, Tan, Atan2, Cartesian, Random)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/function-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Mathematical functions applied to the input: power, two to the power, square root, exponential, natural and base-2 logarithm, absolute value, ceiling, floor, linear interpolation, minimum, maximum, cosine, sine, tangent, arc tangent 2, a cartesian conversion node and a random node.\nuse: Shaping curves, waves and falloffs inside a Pixel Processor or in parameter functions.\ncop: Matching VEX or OpenCL built-ins; without code, @function offers Arithmetic (Absolute, Sign, Normalize, Negate, Reciprocal, Floor, Round, Ceil, Modulus), Powers (Square Root, pow(x, E), pow(b, x), exp(x), Base-Log, Natural Log) and Trigonometry (Sin, Cos, Tan, Arc Sin, Arc Cos, Arc Tan, hyperbolic variants) classes.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-cast-vector-swizzle\nname: Cast, Vector and Swizzle nodes\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/vector-and-swizzle-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Cast nodes convert a value to another type; Vector nodes construct 2, 3 or 4 component vectors (two inputs, ordered), and Swizzle nodes extract components (similar to RGBA Merge and RGBA Split).\nuse: Packing and unpacking positions, colours and directions in a function.\ncop: Vector types and swizzles in VEX (v.xy, set(a, b, c)) or OpenCL; layer-level packing with @channeljoin, @channelsplit and @channelswap.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-constant-nodes\nname: Constant nodes\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/atomic-function-nodes/constant-nodes\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Nodes that provide constant values of the supported types (boolean, integers, floats, vectors, strings...).\nuse: Literal values in function graphs.\ncop: Literals in code, or parameters of the HDA.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-random-nodes\nname: Random and Hash function nodes\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/function-node-library/function-nodes-random\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Library nodes generating random values with different algorithms: global random, normal distribution, random discrete in a range, uniform ranges for floats and vectors, and Hash 11, 14, 21, 22, 24, 31, 32 hash functions (hash functions added in 15.1).\nuse: Random per-pattern or per-pixel variation in FX-Map and Pixel Processor functions.\ncop: The VEX rand and noise functions, hash functions in OpenCL, or without code @randommono and @randomrgb driven by an ID layer or per-pixel seed.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fn-color-nodes\nname: Color function nodes\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/function-node-library/function-nodes-color\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: Library nodes for colour conversions and tonemapping: RGB to and from HSL, HSV, HSI, HCL, ACEScg to linear sRGB, linear to sRGB, lightness, saturation and hue measures, random colour and luminosity, direction to normal, and others.\nuse: Colour processing inside a Pixel Processor.\ncop: @hsv for RGB / HSV conversions, @ociotransform for colour spaces (including ACEScg), @tonemap for tonemapping, VEX colour functions otherwise.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fx-quadrant\nname: Quadrant (FX-Map node)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/fx-maps/the-quadrant-node\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: The most powerful FX-Map node: it subdivides the FX-Map image into four parts for the next node of the chain, is the only node that increases the depth (octave) of the underlying quad-tree, and draws an optional pattern at its position.\nk: Branch Offset | placeholder\nuse: Recursive placement of patterns (multi-scale rock or crack layouts, subdivided tilings, random scatter at several octaves).\nnote: Parameters documented by Adobe: Branch Offset, Pattern (input image or built-in shapes such as Square, Disc, Bell, Gaussian, Thorn, Pyramid, Brick, Waves, Capsule), Pattern Offset, Pattern Size, Pattern Rotation, Pattern Variation, Blending Mode, Random Seed and Inherit Random; most can be driven by a dynamic function graph. A Quadrant node can only render a grayscale input into a grayscale FX-Map and a colour input into a colour FX-Map.\ncop: No single node. A quad-tree subdivision can be generated as points in SOPs (or with @pointgenerate) and stamped with @stamppoint or @shapescatter ({Blend Method}=Maximum), with per-level scale and rotation set by @configurestamps; the recursion logic itself is VEX or Python. UNVERIFIED chain.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fx-iterate\nname: Iterate (FX-Map node)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/fx-maps/the-iterate-node\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: Repeater node of FX-Maps: repeats the received image a chosen number of times, each repeat evaluated separately with its own dynamic function results (the $number variable gives the repeat index); a pass-through connector sends the input image through untouched.\nk: Iterations | placeholder\nuse: Several random copies of a pattern at the same level of the quad-tree (scatter of N stones per cell).\ncop: Copies at the same level with @copyxform ({Total Number}, {Rotate}, {Scale}, {Blend Method}) or random stamps with @shapescatter and {Scatter Seed}.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## fx-switch\nname: Switch (FX-Map node)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/fx-maps/fxmaps\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: low\npurpose: The third FX-Map node type: chooses which branch of the FX-Map graph to follow according to a dynamic function.\nnote: The FX-Map overview names three node types, Quadrant, Iterate and Switch.\nuse: Branching the placement logic (for example different patterns at different octaves).\ncop: Branching in the VEX or Python code that generates the points; @switch for choosing between prepared layers.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## sdf-function-nodes\nname: SDF function nodes (3D primitives, operators, transforms and material setters)\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-function-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-function-graphs/function-node-library/function-nodes-sdf-functions/working-with-sdf-functions\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: medium\npurpose: New in Designer 16.0: a family of function nodes for authoring 3D signed distance functions, with primitives (capped cone, capped torus, capsule, cone, cube, cylinder, ellipsoid, ground plane, helix, hexagonal prism, infinite plane, plane, pyramid, rock, sphere, torus and variants), operators (union, subtraction, intersection and their smooth and chamfer variants, morph, repeat mirror, rounding, shell, symmetry), transforms (bend, elongate, flip, offset, rotate, scale, twist) and material setters (colour, material ID, material, metalness, roughness). They feed Shape splatter v2 and the 3D viewer node.\nuse: Authoring custom 3D stone, bolt or debris shapes that Shape splatter v2 scatters and renders into height, normal and ID outputs.\ncop: Closest in COP: 2D shapes with @sdfshape and combination with @sdfblend ({Mode} Union, Intersect, Subtract, Difference with Smooth, Round and Chamfer fillets) then @planarinflate (SDF to height); true 3D SDFs would be written in VEX or OpenCL.\nstatus: unverified\n\n## sd-pixel-processor-recipe\nname: Pixel Processor to Copernicus recipe\nurl: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/atomic-nodes/pixel-processor\ncat: Function graph nodes\nrel: high\npurpose: Practical translation recipe for a Pixel Processor function, not a node: the per-pixel function graph becomes one OpenCL or VEX kernel, the Sample nodes become reads of the bound input layers, and $pos becomes the pixel coordinate.\nuse: Porting custom Designer filters (slope-based erosion approximations, custom noises, edge masks) to a single COP node.\ncop: Prefer @opencl (all computations stay on the GPU) over @wrangle (a CPU node that can slow a network); the Wrangle COP binds all layers into geometry input 0 and exposes each layer value as @layername; wrap the finished kernel in an HDA and promote its constants as parameters.\nstatus: unverified\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"con