{"parentUuid":"69256583-ef03-49d3-8a53-2f129f43d01a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Podsq3bFVj5zEeg9tLiKFS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Dot node\" Portal Substance 3D Designer graph items experienceleague.adobe.com\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Dot node (also Portal)\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/workspace/graph-view/graph-items/dot-node\"},{\"title\":\"Graph items\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/workspace/graph-view/graph-items/graph-items\"},{\"title\":\"Graph view\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/workspace/graph-view/the-graph-view\"},{\"title\":\"Version 2019.2 (9.2)\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/release-notes/old-versions/version-2019-2-9-2\"},{\"title\":\"Nodes and properties\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/scripting/nodes-and-properties\"},{\"title\":\"Frame\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/workspace/graph-view/graph-items/frame\"},{\"title\":\"Version 13.1\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/release-notes/version-13-1\"},{\"title\":\"Version 13.0\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/release-notes/version-13-0\"},{\"title\":\"All changes\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/release-notes/all-changes\"},{\"title\":\"3D view\",\"url\":\"https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/workspace/3d-view/3d-view\"}]\n\nThe Experience League page for the Dot node (also called Portal) is the top result. It covers how to create, merge, and use Dot nodes as portals in Substance 3D Designer.\n\n**What it is:** A Dot node is a helper for tidying graphs. It reroutes and groups connections, and it helps most in graphs with many long links crossing other links or nodes. A pair of Dot nodes can be used as portals to hide a connection going over a long distance.\n\n**Creating one:** You can add a Dot node in several ways:\n- **On a link:** Holding Alt while hovering a connection shows a preview, and clicking adds the node there.\n- **From a connector:** Holding Alt while dragging from a node connector inserts a Dot node at that spot.\n- **Node menu:** Pressing Spacebar opens the Node menu, where you can search for \"dot.\"\n\n**Merging links:** Holding Alt and moving a Dot node over links merges several connections together.\n\n**Portals:** A portal forms between a transmitter and a receiver Dot node. A portal is automatically created between two Dot nodes – a transmitter and a receiver – when the transmitter Dot node is named. Receivers can be linked by wiring them to a transmitter's output or by picking the transmitter's name in the receiver's Input Portal property. Portal Dot nodes show a wireless signal icon, and using Dot nodes as portals is not supported in FX-Map graphs.\n\n**Graph items context:** The Graph items page lists Dot nodes alongside frames, comments, and pins, which are all tools for organizing and navigating graphs. Frames group nodes under a visible title, and pins mark points of interest you can jump back to.\n\n**Version history:** Dot nodes arrived in 2019.2. The Portal node is a new extension of the Dot node with the possibility to hide connections in your graph. That extension was introduced in version 13.0.\n\nThe search results were all from the Experience League documentation and release notes, and the page metadata showed them as recently updated.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d7eedc89-