{"parentUuid":"b79cab0e-0ae3-4e6f-ba12-5f449488379c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"a9840a94295146d85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LiYGYxmX341nFXNw1aN2AC","type":"tool_result","content":"### Histogram Scan (11 October 2026)\n- Category: Texture filters > Adjustments (11 October 2026)\n- Height relevance: high (11 October 2026)\n- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/adjustments/histogram-scan (11 October 2026)\n- Purpose: Intuitive remap of the brightness and contrast of a grayscale input; it grows or shrinks masks by moving a threshold position and sets how hard the transition is. (11 October 2026)\n- Key parameters: (11 October 2026)\n  - `Position` (0.0 to 1.0): Moves the midpoint of the result (a brightness-like control); on a gradient it expands or shrinks the transition. The documentation warns that 0 gives an all-black result, so start near 0.5. (11 October 2026)\n  - `Contrast` (0.0 to 1.0): Sets how hard the transition is, from soft ramp to hard threshold. (11 October 2026)\n  - `Invert Position` (False/True): Inverts the final result. (11 October 2026)\n- Typical use in a height graph: The most used height node: turn noise into stone shapes or cracks, grow or shrink masks, set the level where a pattern starts to show; a hard Contrast gives binary masks for Flood Fill and Edge Detect. (11 October 2026)\n- Copernicus 22 equivalent: `remap` with **Operation**=Threshold (SideFX lists Histogram nodes under Remap and says to use Threshold like Histogram Scan). **Threshold** plays the role of Position (its direction is inverted: Threshold about 1 minus Position, UNVERIFIED), **Width** is the softness (Contrast 1 corresponds to Width 0, lower Contrast widens it, relation UNVERIFIED), **Threshold When**=Greater, **Output Min**=0 and **Output Max**=1; the invert toggle is a swap of **Output Min** and **Output Max** or `invert`. (11 October 2026)\n- Equivalence status: SideFX-confirmed at node level (the SideFX Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer users page lists this correspondence), parameter setup read from the local Houdini 22.0.368 node help (11 October 2026)\n\n### Histogram Scan Non-Uniform (11 October 2026)\n### Slope Blur (11 October 2026)\n- Category: Texture filters > Blurs (11 October 2026)\n- Height relevance: high (11 October 2026)\n- Source: https://experienceleague.adobe.com/en/docs/substance-3d-designer/using/substance-graphs/nodes-reference-for-substance-graphs/node-library/filters/blurs/slope-blur (11 October 2026)\n- Purpose: High-quality blur whose direction is driven by a grayscale slope map, as if the blur followed the slopes of that map treated as a height map; internally based on Directional Warp. One of the most powerful blurs of Designer, able to chip and weather edges or smear dirt and rust. (11 October 2026)\n- Inputs: Slope (Grayscale Input) (11 October 2026)\n- Key parameters: (11 October 2026)\n  - `Samples` (0 to 32): Number of samples (0 to 32), trading speed for quality. (11 October 2026)\n  - `Intensity` (0.0 to 16.0): Blur amount (0 to 16). (11 October 2026)\n  - `Mode` (Blur, Min, Max): Blending of successive passes: Blur averages, Min eats away existing areas, Max smears white areas outwards. (11 October 2026)\n- Typical use in a height graph: Eroded and chipped edges (Min mode on a binary mask driven by a blurred height), streaks running down slopes, flowing dirt and rust, sedimentary smearing of cracks in a height. (11 October 2026)\n- Copernicus 22 equivalent: `distort` with the Distort by Slope recipe (SideFX-confirmed): `slopedir` turns the slope height into a direction layer wired into the `dir` input input of `distort`, which pushes pixels from high toward low values. **Streak**=Blur, Min or Max matches the SD Mode (Min is described as eroding a shape, Max as adding edge roughness), **Scale** replaces Intensity, **Pixels per Step** and **Maximum Steps** replace Samples. Chain several passes for strong effects. (11 October 2026)\n- Equivalence status: SideFX-confirmed at node level (the SideFX Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer users page lists this correspondence), parameter setup read from the local Houdini 22.0.368 node help (11 October 2026)\n\n### Slope Blur Grayscale (11 October 2026)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"63e48355-4c24-474a-a293-1663e60dcb59","timestamp":"2026-10-11T15:09:04.627Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"e6be0069-6e46-4bef-b2ca-7fb6b32a9ebf","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":22,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b79cab0e-0ae3-4e6f-ba12-5f449488379c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
